dt_emb variant shows the expected performance gains in the 1.2-1.5x range.\n\nFor the dt_emb trained variant, the CIVO effect appears moderate in Cartpole due to its simpler 4-DOF dynamics compared to multi-joint locomotion tasks. The RSSM predicts longer timestep transitions more accurately here, so the fidelity gap that dt_embedding exploits is smaller, resulting in value overestimation that stays below the pathological 1.5x threshold. Across the three seeds, I'm seeing eval scores around 710 with roughly 15% degradation from baseline, and nve peaks in the 1.2-1.4x range that don't sustain. Now checking the dt_embed_dim=0 variant to see how removing the embedding dimension entirely affects performance. Looking at the 1.5x threshold across all three seeds—they all fall below it, so that's 0/3 correct, meaning complete false negatives. This reinforces what I found earlier: the 1.5x threshold isn't universally applicable and needs environment-specific tuning rather than being treated as a one-size-fits-all diagnostic.\n\nNow I'm going to write out the full YAML documentation. This will be comprehensive, so I need to structure it efficiently. Actually, let me first think through the rationale driving each experiment result so I can explain them clearly: the Walker 008d multi-seed case shows stochastic collapse where early training dynamics determine whether the nve spike sustains or self-corrects, and the threshold cleanly separates trained from untrained. The Walker 008f case has no CIVO because there's no dt_embedding, preventing the critic from leveraging dt-conditional information, though performance still varies across runs.\n\nFor Walker 008a, value suppression replicates because the untrained dt_emb blocks the CIVO feedback loop, with a zigzag pattern expected from dt=2 imagination quality fluctuations. Walker 008e_v3 shows collapse in half the seeds but through policy instability rather than CIVO—dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO but the trained dt_emb in the training data creates a lingering vulnerability. The frozen dt_emb experiment reveals gradient starvation where the frozen embedding sends confused signals to the critic, zeroing out gradients through dt_emb while distorting others. Finally, Walker 008e_v3 seeds 3-4 sit under the median tier with Pathway B present but still under-characterized.\n\nMoving to Cheetah, the baseline achieves standard DreamerV3 performance around 700-730. With trained dt_emb, CIVO partially replicates with one-third of seeds collapsing, suggesting environment-specific dynamics modulate CIVO severity. Setting dt_embed_dim to zero eliminates CIVO entirely, consistent with Walker results but with degraded performance from reward prediction issues. Untrained dt_emb partially replicates value suppression, achieving 60-75% of baseline performance. The remaining experiments derive from existing data without additional GPU cost, while Hopper Hop is marked as P2 priority with minimal data under the median tier, and Cartpole Swingup experiments are still being characterized.\n\nNow I'm working through the uncertainty-gated truncation experiment with ambiguous pilot results, and I need to finalize the YAML structure. I'm deciding whether to report per-seed or aggregate results—I'll include both in the output. Let me clarify the untrained dt_emb configuration: the embedding is trained but never exposed to dt>1 data during training.\n\nWriting YAML structure...\n\nFor the Cheetah untrained dt_emb experiment, I'm comparing against Walker 008a which achieved 85% of baseline. The median tier doesn't constrain this, so I should expect similar performance around 80% baseline rather than the lower 77% I initially predicted. Adjusting the per-seed estimates upward to reflect this—seed 0 around 585, seed 1 around 560, seed 2 around 545, with an IQM closer to 563.\n\nBut looking at the actual results showing 79% baseline, I'm reconsidering whether Cheetah's more complex dynamics might pull performance down slightly. The untrained dt_emb introduces noise and the longer H=15 horizon doesn't fully compensate for the dt=2 imagination constraint, so maybe 75-80% is more realistic than the 85% Walker achieved. I'm settling on a prediction around 575 for seed 0 at 80% baseline.\n\nNow I need to write out the YAML configuration. Before I do that, I'm checking the timeline constraints—with CoRL due in 13 days and each Cartpole experiment requiring about 15 GPU hours across 3 seeds, I need to verify whether the planned experiments will finish in time. Writing the YAML configuration now with the median tier setup, including experiment plans, structured tables for key results, figure descriptions, and brief analysis sections for each experiment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41035,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41035},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b3897c87-657f-4445-bad7-6097929bba2b","uuid":"91d317b2-6b9d-4e9b-b78a-8ac462c32424"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XSzGXCjhx8k76QN8niN44h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: |\n  Claims scoped to 'Walker Walk + Cheetah Run with consistent mechanism'. Cartpole either dropped or presented as preliminary with borderline CIVO signal (nve 1.2-1.5x, below diagnostic threshold).\n  NVE 1.5x threshold presented with environment-specific calibration caveat rather than universal diagnostic. Cross-env accuracy ~80% (Walker 100%, Cheetah 67%, Cartpole 50% at 1.5x; improves to ~90%+ at 1.3x).\n  Dual-pathway finding (CIVO Pathway A + non-CIVO Pathway B) mentioned in discussion as 'evidence of deeper fragility' but NOT elevated to core claim — Pathway B remains under-characterized (2/4 seeds, mechanism unclear).\n  C2 strength adjusted: 'reliably triggers' → 'triggers with high probability in locomotion tasks'. Add explicit limitation on environment coverage.\n  TAWM contrast still holds (dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination-based AC). Still submittable to CoRL as diagnostic paper; reviewers likely request revisions on generality.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  # ============================================================\n  # WALKER WALK ADDITIONAL SEEDS\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: \"Replicate CIVO collapse with independent seeds. 009e=seed1, 009b=seed2.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_009e_eval_105K: 131\n      seed1_009e_nve_peak: 4.12\n      seed1_009e_nve_peak_step: 55K\n      seed1_009e_collapse: true\n      seed1_009e_collapse_onset: 35K\n      seed2_009b_eval_105K: 268\n      seed2_009b_nve_peak: 2.05\n      seed2_009b_nve_peak_step: 25K\n      seed2_009b_collapse: false\n      seed2_009b_self_correction: true\n      seed2_009b_nve_105K: 0.38\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2 (50%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: \"2/2 (100%)\"\n      nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained: \"2/2\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Anchored by observed registry data (009e, 009b both DONE).\n      Trained dt_emb reliably induces nve>1.5x (2/2 seeds), confirming C2 diagnostic marker.\n      Collapse is stochastic: seed 1 sustains nve>1.5x across 3+ consecutive checkpoints (feedback loop locks in); seed 2's overestimation is transient (1.62x@45K→1.41x@55K→0.94x@65K), allowing self-correction via real environment returns.\n      The bifurcation depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and replay buffer sampling determine whether overestimation sustains long enough to overwhelm corrective gradients.\n      Under median tier, Walker Walk is the primary environment where CIVO is well-characterized; these results set the benchmark for cross-environment comparison.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"009e (seed 1) vs 009b (seed 2) trajectory comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - {step: 25K, s1_eval: 167, s1_nve: 1.78, s2_eval: 141, s2_nve: 2.05}\n          - {step: 35K, s1_eval: 146, s1_nve: 1.71, s2_eval: 164, s2_nve: 1.24}\n          - {step: 45K, s1_eval: 141, s1_nve: 2.09, s2_eval: 141, s2_nve: 1.62}\n          - {step: 55K, s1_eval: 83, s1_nve: 4.12, s2_eval: 172, s2_nve: 1.41}\n          - {step: 65K, s1_eval: 250, s1_nve: 1.23, s2_eval: 209, s2_nve: 0.94}\n          - {step: 85K, s1_eval: 202, s1_nve: 1.15, s2_eval: 236, s2_nve: 0.51}\n          - {step: 105K, s1_eval: 131, s1_nve: 1.57, s2_eval: 268, s2_nve: 0.38}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Learning curves (eval return) for 008d seed 0 (original), 009e (seed 1), 009b (seed 2) overlaid. Collapse at 55K for seeds 0 & 1; rising trajectory for seed 2.\"\n      - \"NVE trajectory showing 1.5x threshold line. Both seeds cross threshold; seed 2 crosses back down (self-correction), seed 1 sustains above.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Walker Walk CIVO collapse rate = 1/2 independent seeds. nve>1.5x threshold = 2/2 (100% sensitivity for trained dt_emb).\n      Combined with plan_012 untrained data (0/2 exceed): 4/4 perfect separation. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result for C2.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_012\n    description: \"Replicate causal control (no dt_embedding → no CIVO). 009f=seed1, 009c=seed2.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_009f_eval_105K: 470\n      seed1_009f_nve_peak: 0.95\n      seed1_009f_collapse: false\n      seed1_009f_trend: \"zigzag recovery similar to 008f seed 0; value self-corrects\"\n      seed2_009c_eval_105K: 148\n      seed2_009c_nve_peak: 1.26\n      seed2_009c_nve_105K: 1.14\n      seed2_009c_collapse: false\n      seed2_009c_trend: \"peak 359@75K then monotonic decline to 148@105K; no CIVO but persistent decline\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: \"0/2 (0%)\"\n      nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_untrained: \"2/2\"\n      cross_seed_consistency: \"poor — seed 1 recovers (470@105K), seed 2 declines (148@105K)\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      009c anchored by observed registry data (DONE). 009f was killed; re-run expected to follow 008f seed 0 pattern (zigzag recovery to ~470-500).\n      Without dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked: critic cannot exploit dt-conditional information because no dt signal is provided. nve stays below 1.5x for both seeds (peak 0.95 and 1.26).\n      The cross-seed inconsistency (seed 1 recovers, seed 2 declines) reflects reward prediction degradation: dt_embed_dim=0 → reward_MAE=0.747 (75x baseline). The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 008d's 2.78x), creating noisy learning signal whose impact is seed-dependent.\n      C2 causal control conclusion holds: without dt_embedding, nve never exceeds 1.5x (0/2 seeds). But 008f is NOT a recommended practical mitigation due to reward quality trade-off.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"008f/009c/009f NVE comparison at key checkpoints\"\n        rows:\n          - {step: 25K, s0_nve: 1.02, s1_nve: 0.95, s2_nve: 1.26}\n          - {step: 45K, s0_nve: 0.83, s1_nve: 0.75, s2_nve: 0.72}\n          - {step: 65K, s0_nve: 0.37, s1_nve: 0.45, s2_nve: 0.53}\n          - {step: 85K, s0_nve: 0.65, s1_nve: 0.55, s2_nve: 0.60}\n          - {step: 105K, s0_nve: 0.30, s1_nve: 0.40, s2_nve: 1.14}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE trajectories for 008f (3 seeds) all below 1.5x threshold line. Contrast with 008d trained dt_emb (2/2 exceed 1.5x).\"\n    analysis: |\n      All 3 seeds confirm nve < 1.5x (0/3 exceed), providing 6/6 correct classification for untrained configuration combined across plans 011+012.\n      009c's late-stage nve rise to 1.14x@105K is borderline but below threshold, consistent with dt_embed_dim=0 structurally blocking CIVO amplification.\n      The practical unreliability (009c decline) reinforces that 008f serves as a causal control experiment, not a recommended mitigation.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_013\n    description: \"Replicate value growth suppression in untrained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 config.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 720\n      seed1_nve_range: [0.55, 0.85]\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed1_vs_baseline_105K: \"-21%\"\n      seed1_zigzag_pattern: true\n      seed2_eval_105K: 680\n      seed2_nve_range: [0.60, 0.90]\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.70\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_vs_baseline_105K: \"-26%\"\n      seed2_zigzag_pattern: true\n      mean_eval_105K: 726\n      mean_vs_baseline: \"-21%\"\n      nve_structural_suppression_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: |\n      008a seed 0 achieved 778@105K (85% baseline, nve=0.59x) with two V-shaped recovery cycles. Under median tier, additional seeds replicate the structural value suppression pattern but with typical RL variance.\n      The zigzag pattern is expected because untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → never exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO feedback loop while dt=2 imagination still introduces prediction error that periodically degrades the actor, followed by self-correction from real environment data.\n      NVE remains consistently below 1.0x baseline — this is the structural signature of value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb. The suppression strengthens over time (seed 0: 0.93x@55K → 0.59x@105K) rather than converging to baseline, confirming it's a persistent architectural effect, not a transient delay.\n      Seed variance is expected: 680-720@105K range (74-79% baseline) vs seed 0's 778 (85% baseline). One seed may recover more strongly if the zigzag cycles align favorably.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"008a multi-seed summary\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 0, eval_105K: 778, nve_105K: 0.59, vs_baseline: \"-15%\", zigzag_cycles: 2}\n          - {seed: 1, eval_105K: 720, nve_105K: 0.62, vs_baseline: \"-21%\", zigzag_cycles: 2}\n          - {seed: 2, eval_105K: 680, nve_105K: 0.70, vs_baseline: \"-26%\", zigzag_cycles: 1.5}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed overlay of 008a eval curves showing synchronous zigzag pattern. All seeds show dip-recovery cycles with no terminal collapse.\"\n      - \"Three-seed NVE overlay showing consistent suppression below 1.0x from 55K onwards.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Value suppression replicates robustly (3/3 seeds nve < 1.0x, 0/3 collapse). C3 strength elevated to Strong with multi-seed confirmation.\n      Performance variance (~680-778@105K, 74-85% baseline) is within normal RL seed variability. The zigzag pattern is reproducible.\n      Key contrast with 008d (trained dt_emb): 008d 2/2 exceed nve>1.5x; 008a 0/3 exceed 1.0x. Separation is clear and robust.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination mitigation) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_014\n    description: \"Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination with trained dt_emb. Tests whether CIVO is eliminated.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 870\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.80\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed1_pathway: \"healthy\"\n      seed2_eval_105K: 137\n      seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n      seed2_collapse: true\n      seed2_collapse_onset: 95K\n      seed2_pathway: \"Pathway_B (non-CIVO policy instability)\"\n      seed2_actor_entropy_at_collapse: -3.83\n      seed2_actor_entropy_pre_collapse: -5.21\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2 (50%)\"\n      c6_verdict: \"PARTIALLY FALSIFIED\"\n      gate_dt_1_frac: 1.0\n    result_rationale: |\n      Anchored by observed registry data (both seeds DONE).\n      Seed 1 is healthy (869.9@105K, 95% baseline, nve=0.80x). dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO Pathway A: no dt>1 predictions in imagination → no conditioning-induced value exploitation.\n      Seed 2 COLLAPSED (715→452→137, nve=3.78x) despite gate_dt=1.0 throughout. This collapse has a DIFFERENT mechanism than classic CIVO:\n      — 008d CIVO: value explodes → eval declines → entropy decreases (actor over-commits to wrong policy)\n      — 008e_v3_s2: eval crashes FIRST → value lags → entropy INCREASES (policy destabilizes)\n      This is Pathway B: trained dt_emb in training data (subsample ON → dt>1 transitions in replay buffer) creates vulnerability independent of imagination strategy. The dt_emb influences world model learning even when imagination uses dt=1.\n      009a baseline seed 2 is stable (618→596→836) → collapse is config-caused, not seed-fragile.\n      Under median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. 50% collapse rate matches 008d (also 50%), suggesting dt=1 imagination doesn't reduce collapse probability — it changes the pathway.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Pathway A (CIVO) vs Pathway B (non-CIVO) dynamics\"\n        rows:\n          - {feature: \"value_change\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"+130% (60→138)\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"+13% (339→384)\"}\n          - {feature: \"eval_change\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"-43% (146→83)\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"-81% (715→137)\"}\n          - {feature: \"nve_driver\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"value numerator (explosion)\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"eval denominator (crash)\"}\n          - {feature: \"actor_entropy\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88)\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83)\"}\n          - {feature: \"mechanism\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"eval crash → value lag → policy destabilize\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side panels: (a) 008e_v3 seed 1 healthy learning curve vs seed 2 late-stage collapse at 95K. (b) NVE trajectories diverge sharply at 95K. (c) Actor entropy: seed 1 stable, seed 2 rises during collapse.\"\n    analysis: |\n      C6 partially falsified: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient protection. Trained dt_emb creates multiple vulnerability pathways.\n      Pathway B (eval-driven collapse with entropy increase) is qualitatively different from CIVO Pathway A (value-driven collapse with entropy decrease).\n      Under median tier, Pathway B remains under-characterized — 1/2 observation rate with unclear mechanism. Not elevated to core claim; mentioned in discussion as 'evidence of deeper fragility from trained dt_embedding'.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\"\n    plan_id: plan_015\n    description: \"D041 redesign: freeze dt_embedding (requires_grad=False, random init). Tests whether dt_emb TRAINING is necessary for CIVO.\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 204\n      nve_105K: 0.42\n      nve_range: [0.39, 0.47]\n      collapse: false\n      collapse_mechanism: \"gradient_starvation (not CIVO)\"\n      actor_loss_105K: 0.000179\n      actor_entropy_105K: -4.855\n      gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-78%\"\n      verdict: \"NEGATIVE — no CIVO but severe gradient starvation\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Anchored by observed registry data (exp_015b DONE).\n      Frozen dt_emb prevents CIVO (nve 0.39-0.47x, far below 1.5x) because the critic cannot learn to exploit dt-conditional information from a fixed random embedding. This confirms trained dt_emb is a NECESSARY condition for CIVO.\n      However, the frozen embedding introduces gradient starvation: the dt signal is random noise that the model cannot adapt to. Actor loss → 0 (actor receives near-zero gradient signal), eval volatile (80-238, no convergence), performance severely degraded (204@105K, 22% baseline).\n      This result is architecturally expected: the frozen random embedding provides a constant noise input that the model RSSM must route around, wasting representational capacity. The dt=2 imagination still uses the frozen embedding, receiving nonsensical dt conditioning.\n      Contributes to 2×2 ablation matrix: {trained: CIVO risk} × {frozen: gradient starvation} × {absent: reward degradation} × {untrained-unexposed: value suppression}. Each intervention prevents CIVO through a different mechanism with different side effects.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Eval trajectory showing high volatility (80-238) with no convergence pattern, contrasted with 008d's directional collapse and 008a's zigzag recovery.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Confirms trained dt_emb is necessary for CIVO. But frozen dt_emb is not a practical mitigation due to gradient starvation.\n      Combined with 008f (absent dt_emb → reward degradation) and 008a (untrained but trainable → value suppression), the 2×2 matrix shows that ONLY training dt_emb on dt>1 data creates CIVO.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\"\n    plan_id: plan_016\n    description: \"Narrow Pathway B collapse rate estimate with 2 more independent seeds.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed3_eval_105K: 830\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.72\n      seed3_collapse: false\n      seed3_pathway: \"healthy\"\n      seed4_eval_105K: 195\n      seed4_nve_105K: 2.85\n      seed4_collapse: true\n      seed4_collapse_onset: 85K\n      seed4_pathway: \"Pathway_B (tentative)\"\n      seed4_actor_entropy_trend: \"rising during collapse\"\n      total_collapse_rate_4_seeds: \"2/4 (50%)\"\n      pathway_B_characterization: \"under-characterized — entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds, but mechanism remains ambiguous\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Under median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. The 50% collapse rate (1/2 from seeds 1-2) is expected to hold: 1/2 additional seeds collapses.\n      Seed 3 follows the healthy trajectory (similar to seed 1): eval rises smoothly to ~830, nve stays in 0.7-0.8x range. The dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO (Pathway A), and the stochastic Pathway B trigger doesn't fire.\n      Seed 4 collapses late (~85K onset), following the Pathway B signature: eval drops first, value is stable, entropy rises. The onset is later than seed 2 (95K) due to seed-specific training dynamics, but the qualitative pattern is the same.\n      2/4 total collapse rate (50%) is consistent but insufficient to confidently distinguish from 1/3 (33%) or 2/3 (67%) — would need 8+ seeds for tighter bounds. Under median tier, this ambiguity is accepted: Pathway B mentioned in discussion but not core claim.\n      The under-characterization: we observe the entropy ↑ signature consistently, but cannot determine whether Pathway B is caused by (a) dt_emb distorting world model representations, (b) training data dt>1 transitions creating distributional shift, or (c) a subtler interaction. This remains an open question.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"008e_v3 all-seed summary\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 1, eval_105K: 870, nve_105K: 0.80, collapsed: false, pathway: healthy}\n          - {seed: 2, eval_105K: 137, nve_105K: 3.78, collapsed: true, pathway: B}\n          - {seed: 3, eval_105K: 830, nve_105K: 0.72, collapsed: false, pathway: healthy}\n          - {seed: 4, eval_105K: 195, nve_105K: 2.85, collapsed: true, pathway: B}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Four-seed overlay: two healthy trajectories (seeds 1,3 converging ~830-870) and two late-stage collapses (seeds 2,4 at 95K and 85K respectively). Shaded region shows collapse zone.\"\n    analysis: |\n      2/4 collapse rate (50%) = same as 008d trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination. dt=1 imagination does NOT reduce collapse probability.\n      Pathway B entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds → pattern is reproducible but mechanism remains under-characterized.\n      Paper implication: C6 cannot be salvaged as a mitigation claim. Reposition as diagnostic finding about multiple vulnerability pathways.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # CHEETAH RUN (cross-environment — median tier constrains these)\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_020\n    description: \"dt=1 baseline establishing reference performance in second DMC locomotion environment.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_trajectory:\n        5K: 95\n        15K: 230\n        25K: 380\n        35K: 490\n        45K: 555\n        55K: 610\n        65K: 645\n        75K: 670\n        85K: 695\n        95K: 710\n        105K: 720\n      seed0_eval_peak: 755\n      seed0_eval_peak_step: 285K\n      seed0_eval_last10_mean: 738\n      seed1_eval_final: 720\n      seed1_eval_peak: 733\n      seed1_note: \"anchored by running exp_010a (312K, eval 657.9, peak 732.9@265K)\"\n      seed2_eval_final: 695\n      seed2_eval_peak: 725\n      iqm_final: 718\n      iqm_last10: 722\n      ve_ratio_stable: \"~65-70% from 135K+\"\n      ve_ratio_25K: 0.095\n      ve_ratio_35K: 0.210\n      ve_ratio_45K: 0.305\n      ve_ratio_55K: 0.385\n      ve_ratio_65K: 0.490\n      ve_ratio_75K: 0.580\n      ve_ratio_105K: 0.650\n    result_rationale: |\n      Cheetah Run is a standard DMC locomotion benchmark where DreamerV3 typically converges to ~700-750 eval return. Under median tier, baseline final eval is [650, 800].\n      exp_010a (seed 1) is already running at 312K with peak 732.9@265K and current 657.9@305K. Normal fluctuation — DreamerV3 Cheetah curves show more variance post-convergence than Walker Walk. Final last-10 mean expected ~720.\n      Cheetah Run has more complex dynamics than Walker Walk (more joints, higher dimensionality observation space) but lower asymptotic performance in DreamerV3. The RSSM learns Cheetah dynamics adequately but with slightly higher prediction error than Walker due to fast joint movements.\n      v/e ratio convergence is similar to Walker (~65-70%) because the actor-critic architecture handles both environments comparably. Slightly lower than Walker's 69-72% due to Cheetah's higher prediction difficulty.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed summary\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 0, eval_peak: 755, eval_final_10_mean: 738, ve_stable: \"67%\"}\n          - {seed: 1, eval_peak: 733, eval_final_10_mean: 720, ve_stable: \"65%\"}\n          - {seed: 2, eval_peak: 725, eval_final_10_mean: 695, ve_stable: \"66%\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run baseline learning curves (3 seeds) — slower convergence than Walker Walk, higher post-convergence variance, asymptote ~700-750.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Cheetah baseline establishes calibration for cross-environment NVE normalization. Key reference: v/e@35K~0.21, v/e@45K~0.30 — needed to compute nve for trained dt_emb experiments.\n      Lower asymptote than Walker (~720 vs ~950) but comparable v/e dynamics. Cross-environment comparison is valid.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_021\n    description: \"Core cross-environment replication of CIVO. Tests whether trained dt_emb triggers nve>1.5x in Cheetah.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_trajectory:\n        5K: 90\n        15K: 215\n        25K: 350\n        35K: 460\n        45K: 420\n        55K: 340\n        65K: 295\n        75K: 270\n        85K: 310\n        95K: 330\n        105K: 345\n      seed0_nve_trajectory:\n        25K: 0.84\n        35K: 1.24\n        45K: 1.85\n        55K: 2.38\n        65K: 2.12\n        75K: 1.65\n        85K: 1.30\n        95K: 1.08\n        105K: 0.95\n      seed0_collapse: true\n      seed0_collapse_onset: 45K\n      seed0_nve_peak: 2.38\n      seed0_nve_peak_step: 55K\n      seed0_eval_nadir: 270\n      seed0_partial_recovery: true\n      seed0_eval_105K: 345\n      seed0_vs_baseline: \"-52%\"\n      seed1_eval_trajectory:\n        25K: 360\n        35K: 455\n        45K: 510\n        55K: 540\n        65K: 520\n        75K: 545\n        85K: 555\n        95K: 560\n        105K: 568\n      seed1_nve_trajectory:\n        25K: 0.63\n        35K: 0.95\n        45K: 1.18\n        55K: 1.38\n        65K: 1.22\n        75K: 1.08\n        85K: 0.97\n        95K: 0.92\n        105K: 0.88\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.38\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 55K\n      seed1_eval_105K: 568\n      seed1_vs_baseline: \"-21%\"\n      seed2_eval_trajectory:\n        25K: 340\n        35K: 430\n        45K: 470\n        55K: 415\n        65K: 440\n        75K: 480\n        85K: 490\n        95K: 505\n        105K: 510\n      seed2_nve_trajectory:\n        25K: 0.74\n        35K: 1.10\n        45K: 1.47\n        55K: 1.35\n        65K: 1.12\n        75K: 0.93\n        85K: 0.85\n        95K: 0.80\n        105K: 0.78\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.47\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 45K\n      seed2_eval_105K: 510\n      seed2_vs_baseline: \"-29%\"\n      collapse_rate: \"1/3 (33%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: \"1/3 (33%)\"\n      mean_degradation: \"34%\"\n      nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained: \"1/3\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Under median tier, CIVO replicates in Cheetah Run but partially: nve>1.5x in 1/3 seeds (vs Walker's 2/2). Collapse rate 1/3 (vs Walker's 1/2).\n      The weaker CIVO signal in Cheetah has a mechanistic explanation: Cheetah Run's RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is smaller than Walker Walk's. Cheetah dynamics (running gait) are more regular/periodic than Walker (balancing + walking), so the RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately. Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve peaks lower (1.38-1.47x for non-collapsing seeds, vs Walker's 1.62-2.05x).\n      Seed 0 is the exception: early stochasticity pushes nve above 1.5x threshold (peaks 2.38x@55K), triggering the CIVO feedback loop. Even this seed partially recovers (345@105K) unlike Walker 008d's terminal collapse (83@55K), because Cheetah's stronger baseline RSSM predictions provide more corrective signal.\n      Seeds 1 and 2 show borderline nve (1.38x and 1.47x) — tantalizingly close to 1.5x threshold but not exceeding it. This is the key median tier feature: the 1.5x threshold that perfectly separates Walker configurations fails to capture 2/3 Cheetah trained seeds.\n      Mean degradation ~34% (within [15, 45]): collapsed seed 52%, others 21% and 29%.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run 008d-equivalent: per-seed peak NVE and outcome\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 0, nve_peak: 2.38, nve_peak_step: 55K, eval_105K: 345, outcome: \"collapse + partial recovery\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: true}\n          - {seed: 1, nve_peak: 1.38, nve_peak_step: 55K, eval_105K: 568, outcome: \"moderate degradation\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: false}\n          - {seed: 2, nve_peak: 1.47, nve_peak_step: 45K, eval_105K: 510, outcome: \"moderate degradation + self-correction\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: false}\n      - title: \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - {env: \"Walker Walk\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: \"2/2 (100%)\", collapse_rate: \"1/2 (50%)\", mean_nve_peak: 2.07}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah Run\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: \"1/3 (33%)\", collapse_rate: \"1/3 (33%)\", mean_nve_peak: 1.74}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb 3-seed overlay: one collapses at 45K (partial recovery), two plateau. Annotate nve peaks.\"\n      - \"Cross-environment NVE comparison: Walker 2/2 above 1.5x threshold; Cheetah only 1/3 above. Horizontal dashed line at 1.5x.\"\n    analysis: |\n      CIVO mechanism REPLICATES in Cheetah (1/3 collapse, nve-return causal pattern matches Walker) but the 1.5x diagnostic threshold loses sensitivity (1/3 vs 2/2).\n      Two borderline seeds (1.38x, 1.47x) suggest the mechanism operates but at reduced intensity — consistent with Cheetah's smaller RSSM fidelity gap.\n      Paper framing: \"CIVO reliably triggers in locomotion tasks\" is weakened to \"triggers with high probability, environment-dependent severity\". The 1.5x threshold requires environment-specific calibration.\n      Combined accuracy at 1.5x: Walker 4/4, Cheetah 4/6 → 8/10 = 80%. Acceptable for diagnostic paper but reviewers will note non-universality.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_022\n    description: \"Causal control: replicate 008f result (no dt_embedding → no CIVO) in Cheetah.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_105K: 510\n      seed0_nve_peak: 0.78\n      seed0_nve_105K: 0.55\n      seed1_eval_105K: 480\n      seed1_nve_peak: 0.85\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n      seed2_eval_105K: 530\n      seed2_nve_peak: 0.72\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.48\n      collapse_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 507\n      mean_vs_baseline: \"-29%\"\n      reward_MAE_estimate: \"~0.5-0.8 (high, similar to Walker 008f)\"\n      reward_gap_dt1_vs_dt2: \"~1.02x (cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2)\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Without dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked in any environment. The critic receives no dt-conditional information → cannot exploit dt>1 prediction errors.\n      All 3 seeds expected to stay below nve 1.5x (peak range 0.72-0.85x), replicating the Walker 008f pattern. 0/3 exceed threshold → 3/3 correctly classified.\n      Performance degradation (~29% vs baseline) reflects the same reward prediction trade-off seen in Walker 008f: without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap ~1.02x), introducing noise into value learning. This is worse in Cheetah due to the more complex reward structure.\n      Cross-seed consistency expected to be moderate (similar to Walker 008f, where seeds ranged from 148 to 503@105K). Cheetah seeds predicted tighter (480-530) because Cheetah baseline is more stable post-convergence.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-environment dt_embed_dim=0 comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - {env: \"Walker Walk\", seeds: \"3 (008f s0, 009f, 009c)\", nve_range: \"0.30-1.14\", collapse: \"0/3\", eval_range: \"148-503\"}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah Run\", seeds: 3, nve_range: \"0.48-0.62\", collapse: \"0/3\", eval_range: \"480-530\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah dt_embed_dim=0 NVE trajectories (3 seeds) — all well below 1.5x. Less volatile than Walker 008f.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Causal control replicates cleanly: 0/3 exceed nve 1.5x across both environments (0/6 total across Walker + Cheetah).\n      Combined with plan_021 trained result (1/3 exceed 1.5x), Cheetah causal direction confirmed: dt_embedding is necessary for CIVO, even though severity is environment-dependent.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_023\n    description: \"Replicate value growth suppression (C3) in Cheetah.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_105K: 575\n      seed0_nve_105K: 0.65\n      seed0_vs_baseline: \"-20%\"\n      seed1_eval_105K: 535\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.72\n      seed1_vs_baseline: \"-26%\"\n      seed2_eval_105K: 550\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.78\n      seed2_vs_baseline: \"-21%\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 553\n      mean_vs_baseline: \"-23%\"\n      nve_range: [0.55, 0.85]\n      collapse_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      zigzag_pattern: \"present in 2/3 seeds\"\n      value_suppression_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: |\n      Untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → dt_emb trained but never exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO by blocking the learned dt-conditional exploitation pathway. Value growth is suppressed (nve 0.55-0.85x, consistently below 1.0x baseline).\n      Performance reaches ~77% of Cheetah baseline (553/718), slightly below Walker's 85% (778/916). The gap reflects Cheetah's higher observation complexity: untrained dt_emb adds more noise to a system that's already harder to learn.\n      Zigzag recovery pattern (2/3 seeds) replicates the Walker 008a behavior: periodic actor degradation from dt=2 imagination errors, followed by correction from real environment data.\n      Under median tier, C3 replicates in Cheetah with moderate-strong evidence. NVE suppression is the robust cross-environment signal.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah untrained dt_emb eval trajectory — zigzag pattern similar to Walker 008a but lower asymptote.\"\n    analysis: |\n      C3 (value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb) replicates cross-environment. Combined with Walker 008a: 0/6 seeds exceed nve 1.0x.\n      Performance cost (~23% degradation) is moderate — better than trained dt_emb (34% degradation) and comparable to dt_embed_dim=0 (29%).\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # ANALYSIS EXPERIMENTS (0 GPU cost)\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\"\n    plan_id: plan_030\n    description: \"Extend RSSM fidelity analysis to Cheetah Run models.\"\n    expected_results:\n      cheetah_baseline:\n        dt1_obs_mse: 15.8\n        dt2_obs_mse: 28.5\n        obs_gap: 1.80\n        dt1_reward_mae: 0.012\n        dt2_reward_mae: 0.017\n        reward_gap: 1.42\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_collapsed_seed:\n        dt1_obs_mse: 38.5\n        dt2_obs_mse: 48.2\n        obs_gap: 1.25\n        dt1_reward_mae: 0.065\n        dt2_reward_mae: 0.168\n        reward_gap: 2.58\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb:\n        dt1_obs_mse: 9.2\n        dt2_obs_mse: 24.8\n        obs_gap: 2.70\n        dt1_reward_mae: 0.62\n        dt2_reward_mae: 0.63\n        reward_gap: 1.02\n      key_finding: \"Cheetah obs_gap (1.80x) < Walker obs_gap (2.04x) → smaller RSSM fidelity gap → weaker CIVO\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Cheetah Run's RSSM fidelity gap is predicted to be smaller than Walker Walk's (obs_gap 1.80x vs 2.04x). This is because Cheetah's running gait has more periodic/predictable dynamics than Walker's balance-and-walk task.\n      Smaller obs_gap → less dt=2 prediction error → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → fewer seeds crossing the nve>1.5x threshold. This mechanistically explains why CIVO partially replicates in Cheetah (1/3 vs 2/2 in Walker).\n      The trained dt_emb model shows the same pattern as Walker: low obs_gap ratio (1.25x) but high absolute dt1 error (38.5, indicating model degradation), and high reward_gap (2.58x) indicating dt-conditional reward exploitation.\n      No dt_emb model: reward_gap~1.02x (identical to Walker 008f's 1.01x) — architecturally, without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 regardless of environment.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-environment RSSM fidelity comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - {env: \"Walker\", config: \"baseline\", obs_gap: 2.04, reward_gap: 1.56}\n          - {env: \"Walker\", config: \"trained_dtEmb\", obs_gap: 1.30, reward_gap: 2.78}\n          - {env: \"Walker\", config: \"no_dtEmb\", obs_gap: 2.75, reward_gap: 1.01}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah\", config: \"baseline\", obs_gap: 1.80, reward_gap: 1.42}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah\", config: \"trained_dtEmb\", obs_gap: 1.25, reward_gap: 2.58}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah\", config: \"no_dtEmb\", obs_gap: 2.70, reward_gap: 1.02}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"RSSM fidelity gap comparison: bar chart showing obs_gap for baseline models across Walker and Cheetah. Cheetah gap is smaller → explains weaker CIVO.\"\n    analysis: |\n      The RSSM fidelity gap is the mechanistic foundation for CIVO. Cross-environment comparison confirms: smaller gap → weaker CIVO.\n      This provides actionable guidance: environments with more predictable dynamics are less vulnerable to CIVO, because the dt>1 prediction errors that trained dt_emb exploits are smaller.\n\n  - name: \"Actor entropy analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_031\n    description: \"Systematic entropy trajectories for healthy vs collapsed configurations.\"\n    expected_results:\n      healthy_configs_entropy_pattern: \"gradual decrease → stable plateau around -4.5 to -5.5\"\n      civo_collapse_entropy_pattern: \"decrease accelerates before collapse → continues decreasing during collapse (over-commitment)\"\n      pathway_b_entropy_pattern: \"stable decrease → sudden INCREASE during collapse (policy destabilization)\"\n      key_separation_metric: \"entropy change direction at collapse onset\"\n      civo_examples:\n        008d_s0_entropy_55K: -4.88\n        009e_s0_entropy_55K: -4.88\n        016e_entropy_95K: -3.62\n      pathway_b_examples:\n        008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K: -5.21\n        008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K: -3.83\n        delta: \"+1.38 (rising)\"\n      healthy_examples:\n        baseline_entropy_105K: -5.10\n        008a_entropy_105K: -4.95\n    result_rationale: |\n      Actor entropy is a free diagnostic computed from training logs. Three distinct patterns emerge:\n      1. Healthy: entropy gradually decreases as policy sharpens, stabilizes at convergence. Baseline and untrained dt_emb configs follow this.\n      2. CIVO (Pathway A): entropy decreases faster than healthy — the over-valued critic drives the actor to over-commit to specific actions. During collapse, entropy stays low or continues decreasing (policy is confident but wrong).\n      3. Pathway B: entropy suddenly RISES during collapse — policy destabilizes, becoming more random. This is the opposite of CIVO and confirms a different mechanism.\n      The entropy direction at collapse onset is a diagnostic separator: decreasing → CIVO, increasing → Pathway B.\n      Under median tier, this pattern holds but Pathway B has only 2 data points (008e_v3_s2, possibly plan_016 seed 4), insufficient to fully characterize.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Entropy at collapse onset: Pathway A vs Pathway B\"\n        rows:\n          - {experiment: \"008d s0 (CIVO)\", onset_step: 35K, entropy_before: -3.47, entropy_after: -4.88, direction: \"↓ (over-commit)\"}\n          - {experiment: \"016e (CIVO)\", onset_step: 85K, entropy_before: -3.62, entropy_after: -3.62, direction: \"↓ then stable\"}\n          - {experiment: \"008e_v3_s2 (B)\", onset_step: 95K, entropy_before: -5.21, entropy_after: -3.83, direction: \"↑ (destabilize)\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Multi-panel entropy plots: (a) healthy configs (baseline, 008a, 008e_v3 s1), (b) CIVO collapse (008d, 016e), (c) Pathway B (008e_v3 s2). Annotate collapse onset with vertical line.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Entropy analysis provides a zero-cost diagnostic that distinguishes CIVO from Pathway B collapse at collapse onset.\n      Under median tier, the Pathway B entropy ↑ pattern is observed (2 seeds) but not sufficiently characterized to claim as a systematic finding. Include in discussion, not results.\n\n  - name: \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\"\n    plan_id: plan_032\n    description: \"Distributional analysis of V(s) vs Monte Carlo returns.\"\n    expected_results:\n      healthy_distribution: \"unimodal, slight right shift (10-20% overestimation), tight spread\"\n      civo_distribution: \"right-shifted, heavy right tail, mean overestimation 50-100%\"\n      civo_pre_collapse_distribution: \"bimodal — cluster of correct estimates + cluster of severely overestimated states\"\n      untrained_distribution: \"unimodal, slight left shift (5-15% underestimation), wider spread\"\n      key_finding: \"bimodality in V(s) distribution appears 10-15K steps before collapse — potential early warning signal\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      V(s) distribution analysis goes beyond the scalar v/e ratio to reveal HOW value estimates diverge from reality.\n      In healthy configs, V(s) tracks Monte Carlo returns with slight overestimation (10-20%), consistent with DreamerV3's optimistic critic initialization.\n      In CIVO configs, two phases: (1) pre-collapse: bimodal distribution emerges — states visited at dt=1 are correctly valued, states imagined at dt>1 are overestimated. The dt_embedding creates a conditional value surface where dt>1 states have systematically inflated values. (2) post-collapse: the entire distribution shifts right as the actor exploits overestimated regions, corrupting the replay buffer.\n      In untrained dt_emb configs, V(s) is slightly underestimated (nve < 1.0x) with wider spread — the untrained embedding introduces noise that prevents precise estimation in either direction.\n      This is a P2 experiment that would strengthen C2 but is not blocking for submission.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Histograms of V(s) - MC_return at 35K steps: (a) baseline (tight, centered), (b) 008d (right-shifted, heavy tail), (c) 008a (centered, wider spread).\"\n    analysis: |\n      The bimodality finding (if confirmed) would strengthen the mechanistic story: CIVO doesn't uniformly inflate values — it specifically inflates dt>1 imagined states while dt=1 states remain roughly calibrated.\n      Under median tier, this analysis likely completes for Walker but may not extend to Cheetah due to time constraints.\n\n  - name: \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_033\n    description: \"Track learned dt=1 vs dt=2 embedding vectors during training.\"\n    expected_results:\n      initial_cosine_similarity: \"random (~0.0 ± 0.3)\"\n      trained_dtEmb_final_similarity: \"low (~-0.4 to 0.2)\"\n      trained_dtEmb_collapse_similarity: \"divergent (< -0.3) — dt=1 and dt=2 embeddings maximally separated\"\n      untrained_dtEmb_similarity: \"constant at initial value (not trained)\"\n      frozen_dtEmb_similarity: \"constant at initial value (frozen)\"\n      key_finding: \"cosine similarity decreasing correlates with nve increasing — as embeddings diverge, the model creates more distinct (and more exploitable) dt-conditional representations\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      dt_embedding vectors are 8-dimensional (default dt_embed_dim=8). As training progresses, the embedding for dt=2 diverges from dt=1 because the model learns to predict different dynamics at different dt values.\n      In collapsed configs, the embeddings diverge more (lower cosine similarity) because the CIVO feedback loop incentivizes the model to maintain strong dt-conditional differentiation. The critic learns to assign higher values to dt>1 imagined trajectories, and the dt_embedding encodes this distinction more sharply.\n      In self-correcting seeds (009b), the embeddings partially re-converge during self-correction — as the value overestimation decreases, the gradient pressure to maintain sharp dt-distinction weakens.\n      This is a P2 analysis that adds mechanistic depth but isn't essential for submission.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cosine similarity between dt=1 and dt=2 embedding vectors over training steps. Lines for: baseline (constant, no dt>1), 008d s0 (diverging, collapse), 009b s2 (diverging then partial convergence).\"\n    analysis: |\n      Embedding divergence is a consequence, not a cause, of CIVO. But it provides an intuitive visualization of what \"trained dt_embedding\" means mechanistically.\n\n  - name: \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_034\n    description: \"Compute classification accuracy at nve thresholds 1.3x, 1.5x, 1.7x, 2.0x across all seeds and environments.\"\n    expected_results:\n      walker_only:\n        threshold_1_3x: {accuracy: \"4/4 (100%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 0, tn: 2}\n        threshold_1_5x: {accuracy: \"4/4 (100%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 0, tn: 2}\n        threshold_1_7x: {accuracy: \"4/4 (100%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 0, tn: 2}\n        threshold_2_0x: {accuracy: \"4/4 (100%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 0, tn: 2}\n        auroc: 1.0\n      walker_plus_cheetah:\n        threshold_1_3x: {accuracy: \"9/10 (90%)\", tp: 3, fp: 0, fn: 2, tn: 5, note: \"Cheetah seeds 1&2 at 1.38x and 1.47x → classified correctly at 1.3x\"}\n        threshold_1_5x: {accuracy: \"8/10 (80%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 3, tn: 5, note: \"Cheetah seeds 1&2 below 1.5x → false negatives\"}\n        threshold_1_7x: {accuracy: \"7/10 (70%)\", tp: 1, fp: 0, fn: 4, tn: 5, note: \"only Cheetah seed 0 at 2.38x exceeds 1.7x\"}\n        threshold_2_0x: {accuracy: \"7/10 (70%)\", tp: 1, fp: 0, fn: 4, tn: 5}\n        auroc: 0.93\n      all_environments_including_cartpole:\n        threshold_1_3x: {accuracy: \"13/16 (81%)\", note: \"Cartpole 2/3 trained exceed 1.3x (1.35, 1.42; miss 1.22)\"}\n        threshold_1_5x: {accuracy: \"11/16 (69%)\", note: \"Cartpole 0/3 exceed 1.5x → all false negatives\"}\n        threshold_1_7x: {accuracy: \"10/16 (63%)\", note: \"only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 1.7x\"}\n        threshold_2_0x: {accuracy: \"10/16 (63%)\", note: \"only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 2.0x\"}\n        auroc: 0.89\n      optimal_threshold: \"1.3x (cross-environment), 1.5x (locomotion-specific)\"\n      key_finding: \"1.5x threshold has perfect sensitivity in Walker but environment-dependent sensitivity cross-environment. 1.3x improves cross-env accuracy from 80% to 90% with no false positives.\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      0 GPU cost — computed from existing + predicted metrics.\n      Walker Walk: large nve separation (trained peaks 2.05-4.12x vs untrained peaks 0.95-1.26x) gives perfect accuracy at all thresholds. AUROC=1.0 because all trained nve peaks > all untrained nve peaks.\n      Cheetah Run (median tier): narrower separation (trained peaks 1.38-2.38x vs untrained peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 2/3 trained seeds are borderline (1.38x, 1.47x) → false negatives. At 1.3x, these seeds are correctly classified because they exceed 1.3x. AUROC remains high (0.93) because rank ordering is preserved (all trained > all untrained).\n      Cartpole (median tier): narrow separation (trained peaks 1.22-1.42x vs untrained peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 0/3 trained seeds exceed → all false negatives. At 1.3x, 2/3 exceed.\n      Key insight: the nve metric has HIGH discriminative power (AUROC 0.89-0.93 cross-environment) but the specific 1.5x threshold requires environment-dependent calibration. For a universal diagnostic, 1.3x is more appropriate, or use AUROC directly.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Threshold sensitivity matrix\"\n        rows:\n          - {threshold: \"1.3x\", walker_acc: \"100%\", cheetah_acc: \"100%\", cartpole_acc: \"83%\", overall_acc: \"81-90%\"}\n          - {threshold: \"1.5x\", walker_acc: \"100%\", cheetah_acc: \"67%\", cartpole_acc: \"50%\", overall_acc: \"69-80%\"}\n          - {threshold: \"1.7x\", walker_acc: \"100%\", cheetah_acc: \"67%\", cartpole_acc: \"50%\", overall_acc: \"63-70%\"}\n          - {threshold: \"2.0x\", walker_acc: \"100%\", cheetah_acc: \"67%\", cartpole_acc: \"50%\", overall_acc: \"63-70%\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curve for nve threshold across environments. Walker = perfect step function. Cheetah = high AUC but threshold-sensitive. Cartpole = moderate AUC.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Paper should present NVE as a robust diagnostic METRIC (high AUROC) rather than a fixed threshold. The 1.5x value is appropriate for locomotion tasks but environment-specific calibration is needed.\n      Under median tier, recommend presenting AUROC + environment-specific threshold recommendation, with explicit caveat that 1.5x is validated only in Walker Walk.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # CARTPOLE SWINGUP (borderline under median tier)\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_050\n    description: \"dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_trajectory:\n        5K: 45\n        15K: 220\n        25K: 420\n        35K: 580\n        45K: 670\n        55K: 730\n        65K: 770\n        75K: 800\n        85K: 820\n        95K: 830\n        105K: 835\n      seed0_eval_peak: 850\n      seed0_eval_final_10_mean: 838\n      seed1_eval_final_10_mean: 852\n      seed2_eval_final_10_mean: 827\n      iqm_final: 839\n      ve_ratio_stable: \"~67-72%\"\n      ve_ratio_35K: 0.225\n      ve_ratio_45K: 0.320\n      ve_ratio_55K: 0.400\n      ve_ratio_105K: 0.670\n    result_rationale: |\n      Cartpole Swingup is a standard DMC benchmark where DreamerV3 achieves ~820-860 asymptotic return. Simpler dynamics than locomotion tasks (4-DOF pendulum + cart vs multi-joint walker/cheetah).\n      Faster convergence than Walker/Cheetah because: (a) simpler observation space (5D vs 24D for Walker), (b) more regular dynamics (pendulum physics are smooth), (c) reward is dense (based on pole angle).\n      v/e ratio convergence is comparable to locomotion tasks (~67-72%) because the actor-critic architecture is environment-agnostic.\n      Under median tier, these baseline results are expected to be clean and uncontroversial. The interesting question is whether trained dt_emb induces CIVO in this simpler environment.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cartpole Swingup baseline: 3-seed learning curve with faster convergence than locomotion tasks.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Establishes reference for Cartpole NVE normalization. Key difference from locomotion: faster convergence → v/e calibration anchors stabilize earlier.\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_051\n    description: \"Test CIVO in non-locomotion environment. Key question: does simpler dynamics reduce CIVO?\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_trajectory:\n        25K: 390\n        35K: 530\n        45K: 610\n        55K: 660\n        65K: 690\n        75K: 700\n        85K: 710\n        95K: 710\n        105K: 712\n      seed0_nve_trajectory:\n        25K: 0.67\n        35K: 1.00\n        45K: 1.35\n        55K: 1.22\n        65K: 1.05\n        75K: 0.93\n        85K: 0.88\n        95K: 0.85\n        105K: 0.82\n      seed0_nve_peak: 1.35\n      seed0_collapse: false\n      seed0_eval_105K: 712\n      seed0_vs_baseline: \"-15%\"\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.42\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 45K\n      seed1_eval_105K: 685\n      seed1_vs_baseline: \"-18%\"\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.22\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 55K\n      seed2_eval_105K: 740\n      seed2_vs_baseline: \"-11%\"\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      collapse_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      mean_nve_peak: 1.33\n      mean_degradation: \"-15%\"\n      key_finding: \"CIVO mechanism present (nve>1.0x, degradation) but below 1.5x threshold in all seeds\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Under median tier, Cartpole shows nve 1.2-1.5x (borderline). The key mechanism is:\n      Cartpole's RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is SMALLER than locomotion tasks because:\n      (a) Simpler dynamics (4-DOF pendulum) — RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately\n      (b) Smooth observation space — less aliasing at coarser temporal resolution\n      (c) Dense reward — reward prediction at dt=2 is less distorted\n      Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve peaks in 1.22-1.42x range (below 1.5x diagnostic threshold). The trained dt_emb still creates SOME overestimation (all seeds > 1.0x), but insufficient to trigger the CIVO feedback loop.\n      No collapse (0/3) because nve never sustains above the critical threshold where the positive feedback loop locks in. Performance is degraded (~15%) due to the dt=2 transitions in training data adding noise, but the degradation is mild and non-catastrophic.\n      This is the core median tier finding for Cartpole: CIVO mechanism is PRESENT (nve > 1.0x, degradation beyond normal) but NOT DIAGNOSTIC (doesn't exceed 1.5x). The 1.5x threshold is environment-specific, not universal.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cartpole trained dt_emb: per-seed NVE peak and outcome\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 0, nve_peak: 1.35, nve_peak_step: 45K, eval_105K: 712, vs_baseline: \"-15%\", exceeds_1_5x: false}\n          - {seed: 1, nve_peak: 1.42, nve_peak_step: 45K, eval_105K: 685, vs_baseline: \"-18%\", exceeds_1_5x: false}\n          - {seed: 2, nve_peak: 1.22, nve_peak_step: 55K, eval_105K: 740, vs_baseline: \"-11%\", exceeds_1_5x: false}\n      - title: \"Cross-environment NVE peak comparison (trained dt_emb)\"\n        rows:\n          - {env: \"Walker Walk\", mean_nve_peak: 2.07, nve_range: \"1.62-4.12\", exceeds_1_5x: \"2/2 (100%)\", collapse_rate: \"1/2\"}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah Run\", mean_nve_peak: 1.74, nve_range: \"1.38-2.38\", exceeds_1_5x: \"1/3 (33%)\", collapse_rate: \"1/3\"}\n          - {env: \"Cartpole Swingup\", mean_nve_peak: 1.33, nve_range: \"1.22-1.42\", exceeds_1_5x: \"0/3 (0%)\", collapse_rate: \"0/3\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-environment NVE peak distribution: Walker (high, all above 1.5x), Cheetah (mixed), Cartpole (all below 1.5x). Shows monotonic relationship between environment complexity and CIVO severity.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Cartpole confirms that CIVO severity scales with RSSM fidelity gap, which correlates with environment dynamics complexity.\n      For the paper: either (a) drop Cartpole, presenting results only for locomotion tasks where CIVO is clear, or (b) include Cartpole as evidence that the mechanism has environment-dependent severity, with the 1.5x threshold requiring calibration.\n      Under median tier, recommend option (b) with Cartpole as preliminary/supplementary evidence, noting that a lower threshold (~1.3x) would capture the Cartpole signal.\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_052\n    description: \"Causal control for Cartpole.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_105K: 690\n      seed0_nve_peak: 0.82\n      seed1_eval_105K: 670\n      seed1_nve_peak: 0.75\n      seed2_eval_105K: 710\n      seed2_nve_peak: 0.80\n      collapse_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 690\n      mean_vs_baseline: \"-18%\"\n      reward_degradation: \"present but milder than Walker 008f due to simpler reward structure\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Without dt_embedding, CIVO is blocked. All seeds stay below nve 1.5x (peak 0.75-0.82x).\n      Performance degradation (~18%) is from reward prediction noise (no dt conditioning → reward head can't distinguish dt=1/dt=2), same mechanism as Walker 008f.\n      The degradation is comparable to the trained dt_emb condition (~15%), unlike Walker where dt_embed_dim=0 degradation (45%) is much worse than trained dt_emb degradation. In Cartpole, both conditions degrade similarly because the CIVO feedback loop doesn't strongly differentiate them.\n      Under median tier, this confirms the causal control in a third environment: 0/3 seeds exceed nve 1.5x.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cartpole dt_embed_dim=0 NVE: all seeds below 1.0x, confirming causal control.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Combined untrained/absent dt_emb across all environments: 0/11 seeds exceed nve 1.5x (Walker 0/5, Cheetah 0/3, Cartpole 0/3).\n      This is the strongest cross-environment result: CIVO requires trained dt_embedding in every environment tested.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # EXPLORATORY / LOWER PRIORITY\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot (Walker Walk, 1 seed)\"\n    plan_id: plan_060\n    description: \"Truncate imagination when RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds threshold.\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 580\n      nve_105K: 0.95\n      collapse: false\n      vs_baseline: \"-37%\"\n      truncation_frequency: \"~30-40% of imagination steps truncated at dt=2\"\n      effective_horizon: \"~10.5 (vs H=15 nominal)\"\n      mechanism: \"truncation prevents worst-case RSSM predictions from corrupting value estimates\"\n      ambiguity: \"delays CIVO but unclear if prevents — truncation threshold sensitive\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Under median tier, uncertainty-gated truncation is ambiguous: it delays but doesn't clearly prevent collapse, similar to 016e (SGS).\n      The mechanism is sound in principle: truncate imagination when RSSM prediction becomes unreliable, preventing corrupted predictions from influencing critic updates. However:\n      (a) Threshold calibration is environment-specific — too aggressive truncation reduces effective horizon (hurts performance), too lenient doesn't prevent CIVO\n      (b) The RSSM's own uncertainty estimates may be miscalibrated, especially during the training period when CIVO develops\n      (c) The truncation addresses the symptom (bad predictions in imagination) but not the root cause (trained dt_emb creating exploitable conditioning in the critic)\n      Expected outcome: moderate performance (580@105K, 63% baseline), no collapse, but nve hovers near 1.0x — the truncation prevents pathological overestimation but at the cost of learning efficiency.\n      Under median tier, this is reported as \"promising but inconclusive pilot\" in the discussion section. Not elevated to a core result.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Eval curve with truncation rate overlay: higher truncation correlates with lower eval, showing the performance-safety trade-off.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Ambiguous result under median tier. The truncation prevents collapse but degrades performance more than dt=1 imagination (which at least has 50% chance of no-collapse with near-baseline performance).\n      Not included as a recommended mitigation. Mentioned as future work direction.\n\n  - name: \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\"\n    plan_id: plan_040\n    description: \"Third DMC environment for generalization.\"\n    expected_results:\n      status_by_deadline: \"incomplete — only baseline (3 seeds) + trained dt_emb (1-2 seeds) completed\"\n      baseline_eval_final: \"~250 (Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task)\"\n      baseline_ve_stable: \"~55-65%\"\n      trained_dtEmb_partial:\n        seeds_completed: 1\n        nve_peak: 1.45\n        collapse: false\n        eval_degradation: \"-25%\"\n      verdict: \"insufficient data to include in paper\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      P2 priority with 45 GPU-hour budget. Under median tier with 13 days to deadline and GPU contention from P0 experiments, only partial results available.\n      Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task (baseline ~250, significantly lower than Walker/Cheetah). DreamerV3 struggles more with hopping dynamics. CIVO behavior is unpredictable: the low baseline performance means v/e ratios are noisier and less reliable for NVE normalization.\n      The 1-2 seeds of trained dt_emb that complete would show borderline results (nve ~1.45x, similar to Cartpole) but with insufficient statistical power to draw conclusions.\n      Under median tier, Hopper Hop is dropped from the paper or relegated to a one-line mention in the limitations section.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: |\n      Insufficient data under median tier timeline. If included, would weaken rather than strengthen claims due to noisy/inconclusive results.\n      Recommend dropping from paper scope entirely. Focus on Walker + Cheetah (+ Cartpole as supplementary).\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41035,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41035},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b3897c87-657f-4445-bad7-6097929bba2b","uuid":"0d21c31d-eb63-483c-bc54-b9e9bcb24b2f"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":825223,"duration_api_ms":827632,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: |\n  Claims scoped to 'Walker Walk + Cheetah Run with consistent mechanism'. Cartpole either dropped or presented as preliminary with borderline CIVO signal (nve 1.2-1.5x, below diagnostic threshold).\n  NVE 1.5x threshold presented with environment-specific calibration caveat rather than universal diagnostic. Cross-env accuracy ~80% (Walker 100%, Cheetah 67%, Cartpole 50% at 1.5x; improves to ~90%+ at 1.3x).\n  Dual-pathway finding (CIVO Pathway A + non-CIVO Pathway B) mentioned in discussion as 'evidence of deeper fragility' but NOT elevated to core claim — Pathway B remains under-characterized (2/4 seeds, mechanism unclear).\n  C2 strength adjusted: 'reliably triggers' → 'triggers with high probability in locomotion tasks'. Add explicit limitation on environment coverage.\n  TAWM contrast still holds (dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination-based AC). Still submittable to CoRL as diagnostic paper; reviewers likely request revisions on generality.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  # ============================================================\n  # WALKER WALK ADDITIONAL SEEDS\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: \"Replicate CIVO collapse with independent seeds. 009e=seed1, 009b=seed2.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_009e_eval_105K: 131\n      seed1_009e_nve_peak: 4.12\n      seed1_009e_nve_peak_step: 55K\n      seed1_009e_collapse: true\n      seed1_009e_collapse_onset: 35K\n      seed2_009b_eval_105K: 268\n      seed2_009b_nve_peak: 2.05\n      seed2_009b_nve_peak_step: 25K\n      seed2_009b_collapse: false\n      seed2_009b_self_correction: true\n      seed2_009b_nve_105K: 0.38\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2 (50%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: \"2/2 (100%)\"\n      nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained: \"2/2\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Anchored by observed registry data (009e, 009b both DONE).\n      Trained dt_emb reliably induces nve>1.5x (2/2 seeds), confirming C2 diagnostic marker.\n      Collapse is stochastic: seed 1 sustains nve>1.5x across 3+ consecutive checkpoints (feedback loop locks in); seed 2's overestimation is transient (1.62x@45K→1.41x@55K→0.94x@65K), allowing self-correction via real environment returns.\n      The bifurcation depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and replay buffer sampling determine whether overestimation sustains long enough to overwhelm corrective gradients.\n      Under median tier, Walker Walk is the primary environment where CIVO is well-characterized; these results set the benchmark for cross-environment comparison.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"009e (seed 1) vs 009b (seed 2) trajectory comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - {step: 25K, s1_eval: 167, s1_nve: 1.78, s2_eval: 141, s2_nve: 2.05}\n          - {step: 35K, s1_eval: 146, s1_nve: 1.71, s2_eval: 164, s2_nve: 1.24}\n          - {step: 45K, s1_eval: 141, s1_nve: 2.09, s2_eval: 141, s2_nve: 1.62}\n          - {step: 55K, s1_eval: 83, s1_nve: 4.12, s2_eval: 172, s2_nve: 1.41}\n          - {step: 65K, s1_eval: 250, s1_nve: 1.23, s2_eval: 209, s2_nve: 0.94}\n          - {step: 85K, s1_eval: 202, s1_nve: 1.15, s2_eval: 236, s2_nve: 0.51}\n          - {step: 105K, s1_eval: 131, s1_nve: 1.57, s2_eval: 268, s2_nve: 0.38}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Learning curves (eval return) for 008d seed 0 (original), 009e (seed 1), 009b (seed 2) overlaid. Collapse at 55K for seeds 0 & 1; rising trajectory for seed 2.\"\n      - \"NVE trajectory showing 1.5x threshold line. Both seeds cross threshold; seed 2 crosses back down (self-correction), seed 1 sustains above.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Walker Walk CIVO collapse rate = 1/2 independent seeds. nve>1.5x threshold = 2/2 (100% sensitivity for trained dt_emb).\n      Combined with plan_012 untrained data (0/2 exceed): 4/4 perfect separation. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result for C2.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_012\n    description: \"Replicate causal control (no dt_embedding → no CIVO). 009f=seed1, 009c=seed2.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_009f_eval_105K: 470\n      seed1_009f_nve_peak: 0.95\n      seed1_009f_collapse: false\n      seed1_009f_trend: \"zigzag recovery similar to 008f seed 0; value self-corrects\"\n      seed2_009c_eval_105K: 148\n      seed2_009c_nve_peak: 1.26\n      seed2_009c_nve_105K: 1.14\n      seed2_009c_collapse: false\n      seed2_009c_trend: \"peak 359@75K then monotonic decline to 148@105K; no CIVO but persistent decline\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: \"0/2 (0%)\"\n      nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_untrained: \"2/2\"\n      cross_seed_consistency: \"poor — seed 1 recovers (470@105K), seed 2 declines (148@105K)\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      009c anchored by observed registry data (DONE). 009f was killed; re-run expected to follow 008f seed 0 pattern (zigzag recovery to ~470-500).\n      Without dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked: critic cannot exploit dt-conditional information because no dt signal is provided. nve stays below 1.5x for both seeds (peak 0.95 and 1.26).\n      The cross-seed inconsistency (seed 1 recovers, seed 2 declines) reflects reward prediction degradation: dt_embed_dim=0 → reward_MAE=0.747 (75x baseline). The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 008d's 2.78x), creating noisy learning signal whose impact is seed-dependent.\n      C2 causal control conclusion holds: without dt_embedding, nve never exceeds 1.5x (0/2 seeds). But 008f is NOT a recommended practical mitigation due to reward quality trade-off.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"008f/009c/009f NVE comparison at key checkpoints\"\n        rows:\n          - {step: 25K, s0_nve: 1.02, s1_nve: 0.95, s2_nve: 1.26}\n          - {step: 45K, s0_nve: 0.83, s1_nve: 0.75, s2_nve: 0.72}\n          - {step: 65K, s0_nve: 0.37, s1_nve: 0.45, s2_nve: 0.53}\n          - {step: 85K, s0_nve: 0.65, s1_nve: 0.55, s2_nve: 0.60}\n          - {step: 105K, s0_nve: 0.30, s1_nve: 0.40, s2_nve: 1.14}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"NVE trajectories for 008f (3 seeds) all below 1.5x threshold line. Contrast with 008d trained dt_emb (2/2 exceed 1.5x).\"\n    analysis: |\n      All 3 seeds confirm nve < 1.5x (0/3 exceed), providing 6/6 correct classification for untrained configuration combined across plans 011+012.\n      009c's late-stage nve rise to 1.14x@105K is borderline but below threshold, consistent with dt_embed_dim=0 structurally blocking CIVO amplification.\n      The practical unreliability (009c decline) reinforces that 008f serves as a causal control experiment, not a recommended mitigation.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_013\n    description: \"Replicate value growth suppression in untrained dt_emb + subsample OFF + H=15 config.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 720\n      seed1_nve_range: [0.55, 0.85]\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed1_vs_baseline_105K: \"-21%\"\n      seed1_zigzag_pattern: true\n      seed2_eval_105K: 680\n      seed2_nve_range: [0.60, 0.90]\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.70\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_vs_baseline_105K: \"-26%\"\n      seed2_zigzag_pattern: true\n      mean_eval_105K: 726\n      mean_vs_baseline: \"-21%\"\n      nve_structural_suppression_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: |\n      008a seed 0 achieved 778@105K (85% baseline, nve=0.59x) with two V-shaped recovery cycles. Under median tier, additional seeds replicate the structural value suppression pattern but with typical RL variance.\n      The zigzag pattern is expected because untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → never exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO feedback loop while dt=2 imagination still introduces prediction error that periodically degrades the actor, followed by self-correction from real environment data.\n      NVE remains consistently below 1.0x baseline — this is the structural signature of value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb. The suppression strengthens over time (seed 0: 0.93x@55K → 0.59x@105K) rather than converging to baseline, confirming it's a persistent architectural effect, not a transient delay.\n      Seed variance is expected: 680-720@105K range (74-79% baseline) vs seed 0's 778 (85% baseline). One seed may recover more strongly if the zigzag cycles align favorably.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"008a multi-seed summary\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 0, eval_105K: 778, nve_105K: 0.59, vs_baseline: \"-15%\", zigzag_cycles: 2}\n          - {seed: 1, eval_105K: 720, nve_105K: 0.62, vs_baseline: \"-21%\", zigzag_cycles: 2}\n          - {seed: 2, eval_105K: 680, nve_105K: 0.70, vs_baseline: \"-26%\", zigzag_cycles: 1.5}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Three-seed overlay of 008a eval curves showing synchronous zigzag pattern. All seeds show dip-recovery cycles with no terminal collapse.\"\n      - \"Three-seed NVE overlay showing consistent suppression below 1.0x from 55K onwards.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Value suppression replicates robustly (3/3 seeds nve < 1.0x, 0/3 collapse). C3 strength elevated to Strong with multi-seed confirmation.\n      Performance variance (~680-778@105K, 74-85% baseline) is within normal RL seed variability. The zigzag pattern is reproducible.\n      Key contrast with 008d (trained dt_emb): 008d 2/2 exceed nve>1.5x; 008a 0/3 exceed 1.0x. Separation is clear and robust.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (dt=1 imagination mitigation) seeds 1-2\"\n    plan_id: plan_014\n    description: \"Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination with trained dt_emb. Tests whether CIVO is eliminated.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed1_eval_105K: 870\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.80\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed1_pathway: \"healthy\"\n      seed2_eval_105K: 137\n      seed2_nve_105K: 3.78\n      seed2_collapse: true\n      seed2_collapse_onset: 95K\n      seed2_pathway: \"Pathway_B (non-CIVO policy instability)\"\n      seed2_actor_entropy_at_collapse: -3.83\n      seed2_actor_entropy_pre_collapse: -5.21\n      collapse_rate: \"1/2 (50%)\"\n      c6_verdict: \"PARTIALLY FALSIFIED\"\n      gate_dt_1_frac: 1.0\n    result_rationale: |\n      Anchored by observed registry data (both seeds DONE).\n      Seed 1 is healthy (869.9@105K, 95% baseline, nve=0.80x). dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO Pathway A: no dt>1 predictions in imagination → no conditioning-induced value exploitation.\n      Seed 2 COLLAPSED (715→452→137, nve=3.78x) despite gate_dt=1.0 throughout. This collapse has a DIFFERENT mechanism than classic CIVO:\n      — 008d CIVO: value explodes → eval declines → entropy decreases (actor over-commits to wrong policy)\n      — 008e_v3_s2: eval crashes FIRST → value lags → entropy INCREASES (policy destabilizes)\n      This is Pathway B: trained dt_emb in training data (subsample ON → dt>1 transitions in replay buffer) creates vulnerability independent of imagination strategy. The dt_emb influences world model learning even when imagination uses dt=1.\n      009a baseline seed 2 is stable (618→596→836) → collapse is config-caused, not seed-fragile.\n      Under median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. 50% collapse rate matches 008d (also 50%), suggesting dt=1 imagination doesn't reduce collapse probability — it changes the pathway.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Pathway A (CIVO) vs Pathway B (non-CIVO) dynamics\"\n        rows:\n          - {feature: \"value_change\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"+130% (60→138)\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"+13% (339→384)\"}\n          - {feature: \"eval_change\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"-43% (146→83)\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"-81% (715→137)\"}\n          - {feature: \"nve_driver\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"value numerator (explosion)\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"eval denominator (crash)\"}\n          - {feature: \"actor_entropy\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88)\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83)\"}\n          - {feature: \"mechanism\", pathway_A_008d_s1: \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\", pathway_B_008e_v3_s2: \"eval crash → value lag → policy destabilize\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side panels: (a) 008e_v3 seed 1 healthy learning curve vs seed 2 late-stage collapse at 95K. (b) NVE trajectories diverge sharply at 95K. (c) Actor entropy: seed 1 stable, seed 2 rises during collapse.\"\n    analysis: |\n      C6 partially falsified: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient protection. Trained dt_emb creates multiple vulnerability pathways.\n      Pathway B (eval-driven collapse with entropy increase) is qualitatively different from CIVO Pathway A (value-driven collapse with entropy decrease).\n      Under median tier, Pathway B remains under-characterized — 1/2 observation rate with unclear mechanism. Not elevated to core claim; mentioned in discussion as 'evidence of deeper fragility from trained dt_embedding'.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\"\n    plan_id: plan_015\n    description: \"D041 redesign: freeze dt_embedding (requires_grad=False, random init). Tests whether dt_emb TRAINING is necessary for CIVO.\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 204\n      nve_105K: 0.42\n      nve_range: [0.39, 0.47]\n      collapse: false\n      collapse_mechanism: \"gradient_starvation (not CIVO)\"\n      actor_loss_105K: 0.000179\n      actor_entropy_105K: -4.855\n      gate_dt_mean: 2.0\n      vs_baseline_105K: \"-78%\"\n      verdict: \"NEGATIVE — no CIVO but severe gradient starvation\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Anchored by observed registry data (exp_015b DONE).\n      Frozen dt_emb prevents CIVO (nve 0.39-0.47x, far below 1.5x) because the critic cannot learn to exploit dt-conditional information from a fixed random embedding. This confirms trained dt_emb is a NECESSARY condition for CIVO.\n      However, the frozen embedding introduces gradient starvation: the dt signal is random noise that the model cannot adapt to. Actor loss → 0 (actor receives near-zero gradient signal), eval volatile (80-238, no convergence), performance severely degraded (204@105K, 22% baseline).\n      This result is architecturally expected: the frozen random embedding provides a constant noise input that the model RSSM must route around, wasting representational capacity. The dt=2 imagination still uses the frozen embedding, receiving nonsensical dt conditioning.\n      Contributes to 2×2 ablation matrix: {trained: CIVO risk} × {frozen: gradient starvation} × {absent: reward degradation} × {untrained-unexposed: value suppression}. Each intervention prevents CIVO through a different mechanism with different side effects.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Eval trajectory showing high volatility (80-238) with no convergence pattern, contrasted with 008d's directional collapse and 008a's zigzag recovery.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Confirms trained dt_emb is necessary for CIVO. But frozen dt_emb is not a practical mitigation due to gradient starvation.\n      Combined with 008f (absent dt_emb → reward degradation) and 008a (untrained but trainable → value suppression), the 2×2 matrix shows that ONLY training dt_emb on dt>1 data creates CIVO.\n\n  - name: \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\"\n    plan_id: plan_016\n    description: \"Narrow Pathway B collapse rate estimate with 2 more independent seeds.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed3_eval_105K: 830\n      seed3_nve_105K: 0.72\n      seed3_collapse: false\n      seed3_pathway: \"healthy\"\n      seed4_eval_105K: 195\n      seed4_nve_105K: 2.85\n      seed4_collapse: true\n      seed4_collapse_onset: 85K\n      seed4_pathway: \"Pathway_B (tentative)\"\n      seed4_actor_entropy_trend: \"rising during collapse\"\n      total_collapse_rate_4_seeds: \"2/4 (50%)\"\n      pathway_B_characterization: \"under-characterized — entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds, but mechanism remains ambiguous\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Under median tier, Pathway B is present but under-characterized. The 50% collapse rate (1/2 from seeds 1-2) is expected to hold: 1/2 additional seeds collapses.\n      Seed 3 follows the healthy trajectory (similar to seed 1): eval rises smoothly to ~830, nve stays in 0.7-0.8x range. The dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO (Pathway A), and the stochastic Pathway B trigger doesn't fire.\n      Seed 4 collapses late (~85K onset), following the Pathway B signature: eval drops first, value is stable, entropy rises. The onset is later than seed 2 (95K) due to seed-specific training dynamics, but the qualitative pattern is the same.\n      2/4 total collapse rate (50%) is consistent but insufficient to confidently distinguish from 1/3 (33%) or 2/3 (67%) — would need 8+ seeds for tighter bounds. Under median tier, this ambiguity is accepted: Pathway B mentioned in discussion but not core claim.\n      The under-characterization: we observe the entropy ↑ signature consistently, but cannot determine whether Pathway B is caused by (a) dt_emb distorting world model representations, (b) training data dt>1 transitions creating distributional shift, or (c) a subtler interaction. This remains an open question.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"008e_v3 all-seed summary\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 1, eval_105K: 870, nve_105K: 0.80, collapsed: false, pathway: healthy}\n          - {seed: 2, eval_105K: 137, nve_105K: 3.78, collapsed: true, pathway: B}\n          - {seed: 3, eval_105K: 830, nve_105K: 0.72, collapsed: false, pathway: healthy}\n          - {seed: 4, eval_105K: 195, nve_105K: 2.85, collapsed: true, pathway: B}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Four-seed overlay: two healthy trajectories (seeds 1,3 converging ~830-870) and two late-stage collapses (seeds 2,4 at 95K and 85K respectively). Shaded region shows collapse zone.\"\n    analysis: |\n      2/4 collapse rate (50%) = same as 008d trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination. dt=1 imagination does NOT reduce collapse probability.\n      Pathway B entropy ↑ signature consistent across both collapsed seeds → pattern is reproducible but mechanism remains under-characterized.\n      Paper implication: C6 cannot be salvaged as a mitigation claim. Reposition as diagnostic finding about multiple vulnerability pathways.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # CHEETAH RUN (cross-environment — median tier constrains these)\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_020\n    description: \"dt=1 baseline establishing reference performance in second DMC locomotion environment.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_trajectory:\n        5K: 95\n        15K: 230\n        25K: 380\n        35K: 490\n        45K: 555\n        55K: 610\n        65K: 645\n        75K: 670\n        85K: 695\n        95K: 710\n        105K: 720\n      seed0_eval_peak: 755\n      seed0_eval_peak_step: 285K\n      seed0_eval_last10_mean: 738\n      seed1_eval_final: 720\n      seed1_eval_peak: 733\n      seed1_note: \"anchored by running exp_010a (312K, eval 657.9, peak 732.9@265K)\"\n      seed2_eval_final: 695\n      seed2_eval_peak: 725\n      iqm_final: 718\n      iqm_last10: 722\n      ve_ratio_stable: \"~65-70% from 135K+\"\n      ve_ratio_25K: 0.095\n      ve_ratio_35K: 0.210\n      ve_ratio_45K: 0.305\n      ve_ratio_55K: 0.385\n      ve_ratio_65K: 0.490\n      ve_ratio_75K: 0.580\n      ve_ratio_105K: 0.650\n    result_rationale: |\n      Cheetah Run is a standard DMC locomotion benchmark where DreamerV3 typically converges to ~700-750 eval return. Under median tier, baseline final eval is [650, 800].\n      exp_010a (seed 1) is already running at 312K with peak 732.9@265K and current 657.9@305K. Normal fluctuation — DreamerV3 Cheetah curves show more variance post-convergence than Walker Walk. Final last-10 mean expected ~720.\n      Cheetah Run has more complex dynamics than Walker Walk (more joints, higher dimensionality observation space) but lower asymptotic performance in DreamerV3. The RSSM learns Cheetah dynamics adequately but with slightly higher prediction error than Walker due to fast joint movements.\n      v/e ratio convergence is similar to Walker (~65-70%) because the actor-critic architecture handles both environments comparably. Slightly lower than Walker's 69-72% due to Cheetah's higher prediction difficulty.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run baseline 3-seed summary\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 0, eval_peak: 755, eval_final_10_mean: 738, ve_stable: \"67%\"}\n          - {seed: 1, eval_peak: 733, eval_final_10_mean: 720, ve_stable: \"65%\"}\n          - {seed: 2, eval_peak: 725, eval_final_10_mean: 695, ve_stable: \"66%\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run baseline learning curves (3 seeds) — slower convergence than Walker Walk, higher post-convergence variance, asymptote ~700-750.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Cheetah baseline establishes calibration for cross-environment NVE normalization. Key reference: v/e@35K~0.21, v/e@45K~0.30 — needed to compute nve for trained dt_emb experiments.\n      Lower asymptote than Walker (~720 vs ~950) but comparable v/e dynamics. Cross-environment comparison is valid.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_021\n    description: \"Core cross-environment replication of CIVO. Tests whether trained dt_emb triggers nve>1.5x in Cheetah.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_trajectory:\n        5K: 90\n        15K: 215\n        25K: 350\n        35K: 460\n        45K: 420\n        55K: 340\n        65K: 295\n        75K: 270\n        85K: 310\n        95K: 330\n        105K: 345\n      seed0_nve_trajectory:\n        25K: 0.84\n        35K: 1.24\n        45K: 1.85\n        55K: 2.38\n        65K: 2.12\n        75K: 1.65\n        85K: 1.30\n        95K: 1.08\n        105K: 0.95\n      seed0_collapse: true\n      seed0_collapse_onset: 45K\n      seed0_nve_peak: 2.38\n      seed0_nve_peak_step: 55K\n      seed0_eval_nadir: 270\n      seed0_partial_recovery: true\n      seed0_eval_105K: 345\n      seed0_vs_baseline: \"-52%\"\n      seed1_eval_trajectory:\n        25K: 360\n        35K: 455\n        45K: 510\n        55K: 540\n        65K: 520\n        75K: 545\n        85K: 555\n        95K: 560\n        105K: 568\n      seed1_nve_trajectory:\n        25K: 0.63\n        35K: 0.95\n        45K: 1.18\n        55K: 1.38\n        65K: 1.22\n        75K: 1.08\n        85K: 0.97\n        95K: 0.92\n        105K: 0.88\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.38\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 55K\n      seed1_eval_105K: 568\n      seed1_vs_baseline: \"-21%\"\n      seed2_eval_trajectory:\n        25K: 340\n        35K: 430\n        45K: 470\n        55K: 415\n        65K: 440\n        75K: 480\n        85K: 490\n        95K: 505\n        105K: 510\n      seed2_nve_trajectory:\n        25K: 0.74\n        35K: 1.10\n        45K: 1.47\n        55K: 1.35\n        65K: 1.12\n        75K: 0.93\n        85K: 0.85\n        95K: 0.80\n        105K: 0.78\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.47\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 45K\n      seed2_eval_105K: 510\n      seed2_vs_baseline: \"-29%\"\n      collapse_rate: \"1/3 (33%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: \"1/3 (33%)\"\n      mean_degradation: \"34%\"\n      nve_threshold_1_5x_accuracy_trained: \"1/3\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Under median tier, CIVO replicates in Cheetah Run but partially: nve>1.5x in 1/3 seeds (vs Walker's 2/2). Collapse rate 1/3 (vs Walker's 1/2).\n      The weaker CIVO signal in Cheetah has a mechanistic explanation: Cheetah Run's RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is smaller than Walker Walk's. Cheetah dynamics (running gait) are more regular/periodic than Walker (balancing + walking), so the RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately. Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve peaks lower (1.38-1.47x for non-collapsing seeds, vs Walker's 1.62-2.05x).\n      Seed 0 is the exception: early stochasticity pushes nve above 1.5x threshold (peaks 2.38x@55K), triggering the CIVO feedback loop. Even this seed partially recovers (345@105K) unlike Walker 008d's terminal collapse (83@55K), because Cheetah's stronger baseline RSSM predictions provide more corrective signal.\n      Seeds 1 and 2 show borderline nve (1.38x and 1.47x) — tantalizingly close to 1.5x threshold but not exceeding it. This is the key median tier feature: the 1.5x threshold that perfectly separates Walker configurations fails to capture 2/3 Cheetah trained seeds.\n      Mean degradation ~34% (within [15, 45]): collapsed seed 52%, others 21% and 29%.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cheetah Run 008d-equivalent: per-seed peak NVE and outcome\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 0, nve_peak: 2.38, nve_peak_step: 55K, eval_105K: 345, outcome: \"collapse + partial recovery\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: true}\n          - {seed: 1, nve_peak: 1.38, nve_peak_step: 55K, eval_105K: 568, outcome: \"moderate degradation\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: false}\n          - {seed: 2, nve_peak: 1.47, nve_peak_step: 45K, eval_105K: 510, outcome: \"moderate degradation + self-correction\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: false}\n      - title: \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - {env: \"Walker Walk\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: \"2/2 (100%)\", collapse_rate: \"1/2 (50%)\", mean_nve_peak: 2.07}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah Run\", nve_exceeds_1_5x: \"1/3 (33%)\", collapse_rate: \"1/3 (33%)\", mean_nve_peak: 1.74}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb 3-seed overlay: one collapses at 45K (partial recovery), two plateau. Annotate nve peaks.\"\n      - \"Cross-environment NVE comparison: Walker 2/2 above 1.5x threshold; Cheetah only 1/3 above. Horizontal dashed line at 1.5x.\"\n    analysis: |\n      CIVO mechanism REPLICATES in Cheetah (1/3 collapse, nve-return causal pattern matches Walker) but the 1.5x diagnostic threshold loses sensitivity (1/3 vs 2/2).\n      Two borderline seeds (1.38x, 1.47x) suggest the mechanism operates but at reduced intensity — consistent with Cheetah's smaller RSSM fidelity gap.\n      Paper framing: \"CIVO reliably triggers in locomotion tasks\" is weakened to \"triggers with high probability, environment-dependent severity\". The 1.5x threshold requires environment-specific calibration.\n      Combined accuracy at 1.5x: Walker 4/4, Cheetah 4/6 → 8/10 = 80%. Acceptable for diagnostic paper but reviewers will note non-universality.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_022\n    description: \"Causal control: replicate 008f result (no dt_embedding → no CIVO) in Cheetah.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_105K: 510\n      seed0_nve_peak: 0.78\n      seed0_nve_105K: 0.55\n      seed1_eval_105K: 480\n      seed1_nve_peak: 0.85\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.62\n      seed2_eval_105K: 530\n      seed2_nve_peak: 0.72\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.48\n      collapse_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 507\n      mean_vs_baseline: \"-29%\"\n      reward_MAE_estimate: \"~0.5-0.8 (high, similar to Walker 008f)\"\n      reward_gap_dt1_vs_dt2: \"~1.02x (cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2)\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Without dt_embedding, the CIVO feedback loop is architecturally blocked in any environment. The critic receives no dt-conditional information → cannot exploit dt>1 prediction errors.\n      All 3 seeds expected to stay below nve 1.5x (peak range 0.72-0.85x), replicating the Walker 008f pattern. 0/3 exceed threshold → 3/3 correctly classified.\n      Performance degradation (~29% vs baseline) reflects the same reward prediction trade-off seen in Walker 008f: without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 transitions (reward gap ~1.02x), introducing noise into value learning. This is worse in Cheetah due to the more complex reward structure.\n      Cross-seed consistency expected to be moderate (similar to Walker 008f, where seeds ranged from 148 to 503@105K). Cheetah seeds predicted tighter (480-530) because Cheetah baseline is more stable post-convergence.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-environment dt_embed_dim=0 comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - {env: \"Walker Walk\", seeds: \"3 (008f s0, 009f, 009c)\", nve_range: \"0.30-1.14\", collapse: \"0/3\", eval_range: \"148-503\"}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah Run\", seeds: 3, nve_range: \"0.48-0.62\", collapse: \"0/3\", eval_range: \"480-530\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah dt_embed_dim=0 NVE trajectories (3 seeds) — all well below 1.5x. Less volatile than Walker 008f.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Causal control replicates cleanly: 0/3 exceed nve 1.5x across both environments (0/6 total across Walker + Cheetah).\n      Combined with plan_021 trained result (1/3 exceed 1.5x), Cheetah causal direction confirmed: dt_embedding is necessary for CIVO, even though severity is environment-dependent.\n\n  - name: \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_023\n    description: \"Replicate value growth suppression (C3) in Cheetah.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_105K: 575\n      seed0_nve_105K: 0.65\n      seed0_vs_baseline: \"-20%\"\n      seed1_eval_105K: 535\n      seed1_nve_105K: 0.72\n      seed1_vs_baseline: \"-26%\"\n      seed2_eval_105K: 550\n      seed2_nve_105K: 0.78\n      seed2_vs_baseline: \"-21%\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 553\n      mean_vs_baseline: \"-23%\"\n      nve_range: [0.55, 0.85]\n      collapse_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      zigzag_pattern: \"present in 2/3 seeds\"\n      value_suppression_confirmed: true\n    result_rationale: |\n      Untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → dt_emb trained but never exposed to dt>1 data) prevents CIVO by blocking the learned dt-conditional exploitation pathway. Value growth is suppressed (nve 0.55-0.85x, consistently below 1.0x baseline).\n      Performance reaches ~77% of Cheetah baseline (553/718), slightly below Walker's 85% (778/916). The gap reflects Cheetah's higher observation complexity: untrained dt_emb adds more noise to a system that's already harder to learn.\n      Zigzag recovery pattern (2/3 seeds) replicates the Walker 008a behavior: periodic actor degradation from dt=2 imagination errors, followed by correction from real environment data.\n      Under median tier, C3 replicates in Cheetah with moderate-strong evidence. NVE suppression is the robust cross-environment signal.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cheetah untrained dt_emb eval trajectory — zigzag pattern similar to Walker 008a but lower asymptote.\"\n    analysis: |\n      C3 (value growth suppression from untrained dt_emb) replicates cross-environment. Combined with Walker 008a: 0/6 seeds exceed nve 1.0x.\n      Performance cost (~23% degradation) is moderate — better than trained dt_emb (34% degradation) and comparable to dt_embed_dim=0 (29%).\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # ANALYSIS EXPERIMENTS (0 GPU cost)\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\"\n    plan_id: plan_030\n    description: \"Extend RSSM fidelity analysis to Cheetah Run models.\"\n    expected_results:\n      cheetah_baseline:\n        dt1_obs_mse: 15.8\n        dt2_obs_mse: 28.5\n        obs_gap: 1.80\n        dt1_reward_mae: 0.012\n        dt2_reward_mae: 0.017\n        reward_gap: 1.42\n      cheetah_trained_dtEmb_collapsed_seed:\n        dt1_obs_mse: 38.5\n        dt2_obs_mse: 48.2\n        obs_gap: 1.25\n        dt1_reward_mae: 0.065\n        dt2_reward_mae: 0.168\n        reward_gap: 2.58\n      cheetah_no_dtEmb:\n        dt1_obs_mse: 9.2\n        dt2_obs_mse: 24.8\n        obs_gap: 2.70\n        dt1_reward_mae: 0.62\n        dt2_reward_mae: 0.63\n        reward_gap: 1.02\n      key_finding: \"Cheetah obs_gap (1.80x) < Walker obs_gap (2.04x) → smaller RSSM fidelity gap → weaker CIVO\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Cheetah Run's RSSM fidelity gap is predicted to be smaller than Walker Walk's (obs_gap 1.80x vs 2.04x). This is because Cheetah's running gait has more periodic/predictable dynamics than Walker's balance-and-walk task.\n      Smaller obs_gap → less dt=2 prediction error → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → fewer seeds crossing the nve>1.5x threshold. This mechanistically explains why CIVO partially replicates in Cheetah (1/3 vs 2/2 in Walker).\n      The trained dt_emb model shows the same pattern as Walker: low obs_gap ratio (1.25x) but high absolute dt1 error (38.5, indicating model degradation), and high reward_gap (2.58x) indicating dt-conditional reward exploitation.\n      No dt_emb model: reward_gap~1.02x (identical to Walker 008f's 1.01x) — architecturally, without dt_embedding, the reward head cannot distinguish dt=1/dt=2 regardless of environment.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-environment RSSM fidelity comparison\"\n        rows:\n          - {env: \"Walker\", config: \"baseline\", obs_gap: 2.04, reward_gap: 1.56}\n          - {env: \"Walker\", config: \"trained_dtEmb\", obs_gap: 1.30, reward_gap: 2.78}\n          - {env: \"Walker\", config: \"no_dtEmb\", obs_gap: 2.75, reward_gap: 1.01}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah\", config: \"baseline\", obs_gap: 1.80, reward_gap: 1.42}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah\", config: \"trained_dtEmb\", obs_gap: 1.25, reward_gap: 2.58}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah\", config: \"no_dtEmb\", obs_gap: 2.70, reward_gap: 1.02}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"RSSM fidelity gap comparison: bar chart showing obs_gap for baseline models across Walker and Cheetah. Cheetah gap is smaller → explains weaker CIVO.\"\n    analysis: |\n      The RSSM fidelity gap is the mechanistic foundation for CIVO. Cross-environment comparison confirms: smaller gap → weaker CIVO.\n      This provides actionable guidance: environments with more predictable dynamics are less vulnerable to CIVO, because the dt>1 prediction errors that trained dt_emb exploits are smaller.\n\n  - name: \"Actor entropy analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_031\n    description: \"Systematic entropy trajectories for healthy vs collapsed configurations.\"\n    expected_results:\n      healthy_configs_entropy_pattern: \"gradual decrease → stable plateau around -4.5 to -5.5\"\n      civo_collapse_entropy_pattern: \"decrease accelerates before collapse → continues decreasing during collapse (over-commitment)\"\n      pathway_b_entropy_pattern: \"stable decrease → sudden INCREASE during collapse (policy destabilization)\"\n      key_separation_metric: \"entropy change direction at collapse onset\"\n      civo_examples:\n        008d_s0_entropy_55K: -4.88\n        009e_s0_entropy_55K: -4.88\n        016e_entropy_95K: -3.62\n      pathway_b_examples:\n        008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K: -5.21\n        008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K: -3.83\n        delta: \"+1.38 (rising)\"\n      healthy_examples:\n        baseline_entropy_105K: -5.10\n        008a_entropy_105K: -4.95\n    result_rationale: |\n      Actor entropy is a free diagnostic computed from training logs. Three distinct patterns emerge:\n      1. Healthy: entropy gradually decreases as policy sharpens, stabilizes at convergence. Baseline and untrained dt_emb configs follow this.\n      2. CIVO (Pathway A): entropy decreases faster than healthy — the over-valued critic drives the actor to over-commit to specific actions. During collapse, entropy stays low or continues decreasing (policy is confident but wrong).\n      3. Pathway B: entropy suddenly RISES during collapse — policy destabilizes, becoming more random. This is the opposite of CIVO and confirms a different mechanism.\n      The entropy direction at collapse onset is a diagnostic separator: decreasing → CIVO, increasing → Pathway B.\n      Under median tier, this pattern holds but Pathway B has only 2 data points (008e_v3_s2, possibly plan_016 seed 4), insufficient to fully characterize.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Entropy at collapse onset: Pathway A vs Pathway B\"\n        rows:\n          - {experiment: \"008d s0 (CIVO)\", onset_step: 35K, entropy_before: -3.47, entropy_after: -4.88, direction: \"↓ (over-commit)\"}\n          - {experiment: \"016e (CIVO)\", onset_step: 85K, entropy_before: -3.62, entropy_after: -3.62, direction: \"↓ then stable\"}\n          - {experiment: \"008e_v3_s2 (B)\", onset_step: 95K, entropy_before: -5.21, entropy_after: -3.83, direction: \"↑ (destabilize)\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Multi-panel entropy plots: (a) healthy configs (baseline, 008a, 008e_v3 s1), (b) CIVO collapse (008d, 016e), (c) Pathway B (008e_v3 s2). Annotate collapse onset with vertical line.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Entropy analysis provides a zero-cost diagnostic that distinguishes CIVO from Pathway B collapse at collapse onset.\n      Under median tier, the Pathway B entropy ↑ pattern is observed (2 seeds) but not sufficiently characterized to claim as a systematic finding. Include in discussion, not results.\n\n  - name: \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\"\n    plan_id: plan_032\n    description: \"Distributional analysis of V(s) vs Monte Carlo returns.\"\n    expected_results:\n      healthy_distribution: \"unimodal, slight right shift (10-20% overestimation), tight spread\"\n      civo_distribution: \"right-shifted, heavy right tail, mean overestimation 50-100%\"\n      civo_pre_collapse_distribution: \"bimodal — cluster of correct estimates + cluster of severely overestimated states\"\n      untrained_distribution: \"unimodal, slight left shift (5-15% underestimation), wider spread\"\n      key_finding: \"bimodality in V(s) distribution appears 10-15K steps before collapse — potential early warning signal\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      V(s) distribution analysis goes beyond the scalar v/e ratio to reveal HOW value estimates diverge from reality.\n      In healthy configs, V(s) tracks Monte Carlo returns with slight overestimation (10-20%), consistent with DreamerV3's optimistic critic initialization.\n      In CIVO configs, two phases: (1) pre-collapse: bimodal distribution emerges — states visited at dt=1 are correctly valued, states imagined at dt>1 are overestimated. The dt_embedding creates a conditional value surface where dt>1 states have systematically inflated values. (2) post-collapse: the entire distribution shifts right as the actor exploits overestimated regions, corrupting the replay buffer.\n      In untrained dt_emb configs, V(s) is slightly underestimated (nve < 1.0x) with wider spread — the untrained embedding introduces noise that prevents precise estimation in either direction.\n      This is a P2 experiment that would strengthen C2 but is not blocking for submission.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Histograms of V(s) - MC_return at 35K steps: (a) baseline (tight, centered), (b) 008d (right-shifted, heavy tail), (c) 008a (centered, wider spread).\"\n    analysis: |\n      The bimodality finding (if confirmed) would strengthen the mechanistic story: CIVO doesn't uniformly inflate values — it specifically inflates dt>1 imagined states while dt=1 states remain roughly calibrated.\n      Under median tier, this analysis likely completes for Walker but may not extend to Cheetah due to time constraints.\n\n  - name: \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_033\n    description: \"Track learned dt=1 vs dt=2 embedding vectors during training.\"\n    expected_results:\n      initial_cosine_similarity: \"random (~0.0 ± 0.3)\"\n      trained_dtEmb_final_similarity: \"low (~-0.4 to 0.2)\"\n      trained_dtEmb_collapse_similarity: \"divergent (< -0.3) — dt=1 and dt=2 embeddings maximally separated\"\n      untrained_dtEmb_similarity: \"constant at initial value (not trained)\"\n      frozen_dtEmb_similarity: \"constant at initial value (frozen)\"\n      key_finding: \"cosine similarity decreasing correlates with nve increasing — as embeddings diverge, the model creates more distinct (and more exploitable) dt-conditional representations\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      dt_embedding vectors are 8-dimensional (default dt_embed_dim=8). As training progresses, the embedding for dt=2 diverges from dt=1 because the model learns to predict different dynamics at different dt values.\n      In collapsed configs, the embeddings diverge more (lower cosine similarity) because the CIVO feedback loop incentivizes the model to maintain strong dt-conditional differentiation. The critic learns to assign higher values to dt>1 imagined trajectories, and the dt_embedding encodes this distinction more sharply.\n      In self-correcting seeds (009b), the embeddings partially re-converge during self-correction — as the value overestimation decreases, the gradient pressure to maintain sharp dt-distinction weakens.\n      This is a P2 analysis that adds mechanistic depth but isn't essential for submission.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cosine similarity between dt=1 and dt=2 embedding vectors over training steps. Lines for: baseline (constant, no dt>1), 008d s0 (diverging, collapse), 009b s2 (diverging then partial convergence).\"\n    analysis: |\n      Embedding divergence is a consequence, not a cause, of CIVO. But it provides an intuitive visualization of what \"trained dt_embedding\" means mechanistically.\n\n  - name: \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\"\n    plan_id: plan_034\n    description: \"Compute classification accuracy at nve thresholds 1.3x, 1.5x, 1.7x, 2.0x across all seeds and environments.\"\n    expected_results:\n      walker_only:\n        threshold_1_3x: {accuracy: \"4/4 (100%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 0, tn: 2}\n        threshold_1_5x: {accuracy: \"4/4 (100%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 0, tn: 2}\n        threshold_1_7x: {accuracy: \"4/4 (100%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 0, tn: 2}\n        threshold_2_0x: {accuracy: \"4/4 (100%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 0, tn: 2}\n        auroc: 1.0\n      walker_plus_cheetah:\n        threshold_1_3x: {accuracy: \"9/10 (90%)\", tp: 3, fp: 0, fn: 2, tn: 5, note: \"Cheetah seeds 1&2 at 1.38x and 1.47x → classified correctly at 1.3x\"}\n        threshold_1_5x: {accuracy: \"8/10 (80%)\", tp: 2, fp: 0, fn: 3, tn: 5, note: \"Cheetah seeds 1&2 below 1.5x → false negatives\"}\n        threshold_1_7x: {accuracy: \"7/10 (70%)\", tp: 1, fp: 0, fn: 4, tn: 5, note: \"only Cheetah seed 0 at 2.38x exceeds 1.7x\"}\n        threshold_2_0x: {accuracy: \"7/10 (70%)\", tp: 1, fp: 0, fn: 4, tn: 5}\n        auroc: 0.93\n      all_environments_including_cartpole:\n        threshold_1_3x: {accuracy: \"13/16 (81%)\", note: \"Cartpole 2/3 trained exceed 1.3x (1.35, 1.42; miss 1.22)\"}\n        threshold_1_5x: {accuracy: \"11/16 (69%)\", note: \"Cartpole 0/3 exceed 1.5x → all false negatives\"}\n        threshold_1_7x: {accuracy: \"10/16 (63%)\", note: \"only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 1.7x\"}\n        threshold_2_0x: {accuracy: \"10/16 (63%)\", note: \"only Walker 2/2 + Cheetah 1/3 exceed 2.0x\"}\n        auroc: 0.89\n      optimal_threshold: \"1.3x (cross-environment), 1.5x (locomotion-specific)\"\n      key_finding: \"1.5x threshold has perfect sensitivity in Walker but environment-dependent sensitivity cross-environment. 1.3x improves cross-env accuracy from 80% to 90% with no false positives.\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      0 GPU cost — computed from existing + predicted metrics.\n      Walker Walk: large nve separation (trained peaks 2.05-4.12x vs untrained peaks 0.95-1.26x) gives perfect accuracy at all thresholds. AUROC=1.0 because all trained nve peaks > all untrained nve peaks.\n      Cheetah Run (median tier): narrower separation (trained peaks 1.38-2.38x vs untrained peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 2/3 trained seeds are borderline (1.38x, 1.47x) → false negatives. At 1.3x, these seeds are correctly classified because they exceed 1.3x. AUROC remains high (0.93) because rank ordering is preserved (all trained > all untrained).\n      Cartpole (median tier): narrow separation (trained peaks 1.22-1.42x vs untrained peaks 0.72-0.85x). At 1.5x, 0/3 trained seeds exceed → all false negatives. At 1.3x, 2/3 exceed.\n      Key insight: the nve metric has HIGH discriminative power (AUROC 0.89-0.93 cross-environment) but the specific 1.5x threshold requires environment-dependent calibration. For a universal diagnostic, 1.3x is more appropriate, or use AUROC directly.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Threshold sensitivity matrix\"\n        rows:\n          - {threshold: \"1.3x\", walker_acc: \"100%\", cheetah_acc: \"100%\", cartpole_acc: \"83%\", overall_acc: \"81-90%\"}\n          - {threshold: \"1.5x\", walker_acc: \"100%\", cheetah_acc: \"67%\", cartpole_acc: \"50%\", overall_acc: \"69-80%\"}\n          - {threshold: \"1.7x\", walker_acc: \"100%\", cheetah_acc: \"67%\", cartpole_acc: \"50%\", overall_acc: \"63-70%\"}\n          - {threshold: \"2.0x\", walker_acc: \"100%\", cheetah_acc: \"67%\", cartpole_acc: \"50%\", overall_acc: \"63-70%\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curve for nve threshold across environments. Walker = perfect step function. Cheetah = high AUC but threshold-sensitive. Cartpole = moderate AUC.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Paper should present NVE as a robust diagnostic METRIC (high AUROC) rather than a fixed threshold. The 1.5x value is appropriate for locomotion tasks but environment-specific calibration is needed.\n      Under median tier, recommend presenting AUROC + environment-specific threshold recommendation, with explicit caveat that 1.5x is validated only in Walker Walk.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # CARTPOLE SWINGUP (borderline under median tier)\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_050\n    description: \"dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_trajectory:\n        5K: 45\n        15K: 220\n        25K: 420\n        35K: 580\n        45K: 670\n        55K: 730\n        65K: 770\n        75K: 800\n        85K: 820\n        95K: 830\n        105K: 835\n      seed0_eval_peak: 850\n      seed0_eval_final_10_mean: 838\n      seed1_eval_final_10_mean: 852\n      seed2_eval_final_10_mean: 827\n      iqm_final: 839\n      ve_ratio_stable: \"~67-72%\"\n      ve_ratio_35K: 0.225\n      ve_ratio_45K: 0.320\n      ve_ratio_55K: 0.400\n      ve_ratio_105K: 0.670\n    result_rationale: |\n      Cartpole Swingup is a standard DMC benchmark where DreamerV3 achieves ~820-860 asymptotic return. Simpler dynamics than locomotion tasks (4-DOF pendulum + cart vs multi-joint walker/cheetah).\n      Faster convergence than Walker/Cheetah because: (a) simpler observation space (5D vs 24D for Walker), (b) more regular dynamics (pendulum physics are smooth), (c) reward is dense (based on pole angle).\n      v/e ratio convergence is comparable to locomotion tasks (~67-72%) because the actor-critic architecture is environment-agnostic.\n      Under median tier, these baseline results are expected to be clean and uncontroversial. The interesting question is whether trained dt_emb induces CIVO in this simpler environment.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cartpole Swingup baseline: 3-seed learning curve with faster convergence than locomotion tasks.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Establishes reference for Cartpole NVE normalization. Key difference from locomotion: faster convergence → v/e calibration anchors stabilize earlier.\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_051\n    description: \"Test CIVO in non-locomotion environment. Key question: does simpler dynamics reduce CIVO?\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_trajectory:\n        25K: 390\n        35K: 530\n        45K: 610\n        55K: 660\n        65K: 690\n        75K: 700\n        85K: 710\n        95K: 710\n        105K: 712\n      seed0_nve_trajectory:\n        25K: 0.67\n        35K: 1.00\n        45K: 1.35\n        55K: 1.22\n        65K: 1.05\n        75K: 0.93\n        85K: 0.88\n        95K: 0.85\n        105K: 0.82\n      seed0_nve_peak: 1.35\n      seed0_collapse: false\n      seed0_eval_105K: 712\n      seed0_vs_baseline: \"-15%\"\n      seed1_nve_peak: 1.42\n      seed1_nve_peak_step: 45K\n      seed1_eval_105K: 685\n      seed1_vs_baseline: \"-18%\"\n      seed1_collapse: false\n      seed2_nve_peak: 1.22\n      seed2_nve_peak_step: 55K\n      seed2_eval_105K: 740\n      seed2_vs_baseline: \"-11%\"\n      seed2_collapse: false\n      collapse_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      mean_nve_peak: 1.33\n      mean_degradation: \"-15%\"\n      key_finding: \"CIVO mechanism present (nve>1.0x, degradation) but below 1.5x threshold in all seeds\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Under median tier, Cartpole shows nve 1.2-1.5x (borderline). The key mechanism is:\n      Cartpole's RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is SMALLER than locomotion tasks because:\n      (a) Simpler dynamics (4-DOF pendulum) — RSSM predicts dt=2 transitions more accurately\n      (b) Smooth observation space — less aliasing at coarser temporal resolution\n      (c) Dense reward — reward prediction at dt=2 is less distorted\n      Smaller fidelity gap → smaller conditioning-induced value overestimation → nve peaks in 1.22-1.42x range (below 1.5x diagnostic threshold). The trained dt_emb still creates SOME overestimation (all seeds > 1.0x), but insufficient to trigger the CIVO feedback loop.\n      No collapse (0/3) because nve never sustains above the critical threshold where the positive feedback loop locks in. Performance is degraded (~15%) due to the dt=2 transitions in training data adding noise, but the degradation is mild and non-catastrophic.\n      This is the core median tier finding for Cartpole: CIVO mechanism is PRESENT (nve > 1.0x, degradation beyond normal) but NOT DIAGNOSTIC (doesn't exceed 1.5x). The 1.5x threshold is environment-specific, not universal.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cartpole trained dt_emb: per-seed NVE peak and outcome\"\n        rows:\n          - {seed: 0, nve_peak: 1.35, nve_peak_step: 45K, eval_105K: 712, vs_baseline: \"-15%\", exceeds_1_5x: false}\n          - {seed: 1, nve_peak: 1.42, nve_peak_step: 45K, eval_105K: 685, vs_baseline: \"-18%\", exceeds_1_5x: false}\n          - {seed: 2, nve_peak: 1.22, nve_peak_step: 55K, eval_105K: 740, vs_baseline: \"-11%\", exceeds_1_5x: false}\n      - title: \"Cross-environment NVE peak comparison (trained dt_emb)\"\n        rows:\n          - {env: \"Walker Walk\", mean_nve_peak: 2.07, nve_range: \"1.62-4.12\", exceeds_1_5x: \"2/2 (100%)\", collapse_rate: \"1/2\"}\n          - {env: \"Cheetah Run\", mean_nve_peak: 1.74, nve_range: \"1.38-2.38\", exceeds_1_5x: \"1/3 (33%)\", collapse_rate: \"1/3\"}\n          - {env: \"Cartpole Swingup\", mean_nve_peak: 1.33, nve_range: \"1.22-1.42\", exceeds_1_5x: \"0/3 (0%)\", collapse_rate: \"0/3\"}\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cross-environment NVE peak distribution: Walker (high, all above 1.5x), Cheetah (mixed), Cartpole (all below 1.5x). Shows monotonic relationship between environment complexity and CIVO severity.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Cartpole confirms that CIVO severity scales with RSSM fidelity gap, which correlates with environment dynamics complexity.\n      For the paper: either (a) drop Cartpole, presenting results only for locomotion tasks where CIVO is clear, or (b) include Cartpole as evidence that the mechanism has environment-dependent severity, with the 1.5x threshold requiring calibration.\n      Under median tier, recommend option (b) with Cartpole as preliminary/supplementary evidence, noting that a lower threshold (~1.3x) would capture the Cartpole signal.\n\n  - name: \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\"\n    plan_id: plan_052\n    description: \"Causal control for Cartpole.\"\n    expected_results:\n      seed0_eval_105K: 690\n      seed0_nve_peak: 0.82\n      seed1_eval_105K: 670\n      seed1_nve_peak: 0.75\n      seed2_eval_105K: 710\n      seed2_nve_peak: 0.80\n      collapse_rate: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      nve_exceeds_1_5x: \"0/3 (0%)\"\n      mean_eval_105K: 690\n      mean_vs_baseline: \"-18%\"\n      reward_degradation: \"present but milder than Walker 008f due to simpler reward structure\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Without dt_embedding, CIVO is blocked. All seeds stay below nve 1.5x (peak 0.75-0.82x).\n      Performance degradation (~18%) is from reward prediction noise (no dt conditioning → reward head can't distinguish dt=1/dt=2), same mechanism as Walker 008f.\n      The degradation is comparable to the trained dt_emb condition (~15%), unlike Walker where dt_embed_dim=0 degradation (45%) is much worse than trained dt_emb degradation. In Cartpole, both conditions degrade similarly because the CIVO feedback loop doesn't strongly differentiate them.\n      Under median tier, this confirms the causal control in a third environment: 0/3 seeds exceed nve 1.5x.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Cartpole dt_embed_dim=0 NVE: all seeds below 1.0x, confirming causal control.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Combined untrained/absent dt_emb across all environments: 0/11 seeds exceed nve 1.5x (Walker 0/5, Cheetah 0/3, Cartpole 0/3).\n      This is the strongest cross-environment result: CIVO requires trained dt_embedding in every environment tested.\n\n  # ============================================================\n  # EXPLORATORY / LOWER PRIORITY\n  # ============================================================\n  - name: \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot (Walker Walk, 1 seed)\"\n    plan_id: plan_060\n    description: \"Truncate imagination when RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds threshold.\"\n    expected_results:\n      eval_105K: 580\n      nve_105K: 0.95\n      collapse: false\n      vs_baseline: \"-37%\"\n      truncation_frequency: \"~30-40% of imagination steps truncated at dt=2\"\n      effective_horizon: \"~10.5 (vs H=15 nominal)\"\n      mechanism: \"truncation prevents worst-case RSSM predictions from corrupting value estimates\"\n      ambiguity: \"delays CIVO but unclear if prevents — truncation threshold sensitive\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      Under median tier, uncertainty-gated truncation is ambiguous: it delays but doesn't clearly prevent collapse, similar to 016e (SGS).\n      The mechanism is sound in principle: truncate imagination when RSSM prediction becomes unreliable, preventing corrupted predictions from influencing critic updates. However:\n      (a) Threshold calibration is environment-specific — too aggressive truncation reduces effective horizon (hurts performance), too lenient doesn't prevent CIVO\n      (b) The RSSM's own uncertainty estimates may be miscalibrated, especially during the training period when CIVO develops\n      (c) The truncation addresses the symptom (bad predictions in imagination) but not the root cause (trained dt_emb creating exploitable conditioning in the critic)\n      Expected outcome: moderate performance (580@105K, 63% baseline), no collapse, but nve hovers near 1.0x — the truncation prevents pathological overestimation but at the cost of learning efficiency.\n      Under median tier, this is reported as \"promising but inconclusive pilot\" in the discussion section. Not elevated to a core result.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Eval curve with truncation rate overlay: higher truncation correlates with lower eval, showing the performance-safety trade-off.\"\n    analysis: |\n      Ambiguous result under median tier. The truncation prevents collapse but degrades performance more than dt=1 imagination (which at least has 50% chance of no-collapse with near-baseline performance).\n      Not included as a recommended mitigation. Mentioned as future work direction.\n\n  - name: \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\"\n    plan_id: plan_040\n    description: \"Third DMC environment for generalization.\"\n    expected_results:\n      status_by_deadline: \"incomplete — only baseline (3 seeds) + trained dt_emb (1-2 seeds) completed\"\n      baseline_eval_final: \"~250 (Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task)\"\n      baseline_ve_stable: \"~55-65%\"\n      trained_dtEmb_partial:\n        seeds_completed: 1\n        nve_peak: 1.45\n        collapse: false\n        eval_degradation: \"-25%\"\n      verdict: \"insufficient data to include in paper\"\n    result_rationale: |\n      P2 priority with 45 GPU-hour budget. Under median tier with 13 days to deadline and GPU contention from P0 experiments, only partial results available.\n      Hopper Hop is a difficult DMC task (baseline ~250, significantly lower than Walker/Cheetah). DreamerV3 struggles more with hopping dynamics. CIVO behavior is unpredictable: the low baseline performance means v/e ratios are noisier and less reliable for NVE normalization.\n      The 1-2 seeds of trained dt_emb that complete would show borderline results (nve ~1.45x, similar to Cartpole) but with insufficient statistical power to draw conclusions.\n      Under median tier, Hopper Hop is dropped from the paper or relegated to a one-line mention in the limitations section.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: |\n      Insufficient data under median tier timeline. If included, would weaken rather than strengthen claims due to noisy/inconclusive results.\n      Recommend dropping from paper scope entirely. Focus on Walker + Cheetah (+ Cartpole as supplementary).\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"b3897c87-657f-4445-bad7-6097929bba2b","total_cost_usd":1.25867425,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41035,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"output_tokens":38431,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41035,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":38431,"cache_read_input_tokens":11447,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41035,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41035},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":35592,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.035697,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":38431,"cacheReadInputTokens":11447,"cacheCreationInputTokens":41035,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.22297725,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"3edd7196-29be-43ee-9a4b-984776a17600"}
